  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002C",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C64Z9NB800T",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002D",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Symptom: `aperas_apeiron_snapshot_A.bundle` (made on Host A) and `aperas_apeiron_snapshot_B.bundle` (made on Host B) each verify cleanly **only on their own origin machine**, and fail deterministically with `error(unknown_layer_reference(<hash>))` on the other — a different specific missing-layer hash each time.",
    "title": "00C64Z9NB800V",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002E",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C64Z9NB800W",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002F",
    "children": [],
    "text": "An earlier pass at this investigation, run on a single host in isolation, found a byte pattern in a snapshot that looked like Latin-1/UTF-8 mojibake corruption (reversing it produced a plausible gzip header) and concluded the file was corrupted. **That conclusion was wrong and has been retracted** — the same file was later shown to unbundle perfectly on its own origin host, which a genuinely corrupted file cannot do. The byte pattern was a coincidental artifact, not evidence of corruption.",
    "title": "00C64Z9NB800X",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002G",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C64Z9NB800Y",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002H",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Ruled out version mismatch: both machines confirmed running byte-identical `TerminusDB v12.0.7 (57f2093baeafd65e16004e84b7b58e0c5cf72858)` / `terminusdb-store v0.19.8`.",
    "title": "00C64Z9NB800Z",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002J",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C64Z9NB8010",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002K",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Ruled out the `auto-optimize` community plugin (`/plugins/auto-optimize.pl`, a probabilistic post-commit layer squash/GC): disabled on both machines (moved the plugin file out, restarted), re-tested with a brand-new minimal scratch database — still failed cross-host identically.",
    "title": "00C64Z9NB8011",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002M",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C64Z9NB8012",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002N",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Ruled out store \"age\": reproduced with a store `store init`'d seconds before the test, same as the destination.",
    "title": "00C64Z9NB8013",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002P",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C64Z9NB8014",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002Q",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Decisive isolation, no second machine needed**: ran two independent TerminusDB containers (`terminusdb` and a throwaway `terminusdb-freshtest`, same image, same physical host, plugin off on both) and bundled/unbundled between them. Same failure. A positive control — unbundling the same file back into a *different database within its own originating container* — succeeded every time. This isolates the variable precisely: **it's not host A vs. host B, or even separate machines at all — it's any two independent TerminusDB stores (server instances/storage directories), even side-by-side on one host.**",
    "title": "00C64Z9NB8015",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002R",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C64Z9NB8016",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002S",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Cross-checked against prior art: [terminusdb/terminusdb#1147](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/issues/1147) reports the identical symptom moving a bundle between two instances, never reproduced by the maintainer, closed stale/not-planned. [terminusdb/terminusdb#1235](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/pull/1235) (\"an initial effort to figure out #1147 without success\") added CLI bundle/unbundle tests that call `store.init()` exactly once and share that one store across every test — so its test suite never actually exercises the cross-store case, which is the one that fails.",
    "title": "00C64Z9NB8017",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002T",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C64Z9NB8018",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002V",
    "children": [],
    "text": "The official [\"move a database between servers\" backup/restore guide](https://terminusdb.org/docs/enterprise-backup-restore/) is scoped to **Enterprise edition** (different mechanism: `/api/bundle` + `/api/unbundle` REST endpoints, target pre-created) — not a documented guarantee for the Community-edition CLI commands we're using.",
    "title": "00C64Z9NB8019",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002W",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C64Z9NB801A",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002X",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Filed as [terminusdb/terminusdb#2509](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/issues/2509), including the two-store repro and a note about the test-coverage gap in #1235. Draft/record kept at `terminusdb-issue-bundle-cross-store.md`.",
    "title": "00C64Z9NB801B",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002Y",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C64Z9NB801C",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YXFT002Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YXFT002Z",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Conclusion for this project: `bundle`/`unbundle`/`restore.sh` remain valid for same-store snapshot/rollback, but are not to be relied on for cross-machine transfer.",
    "title": "00C64Z9NB801D",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YY0C000J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YY0C000J",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Info:**",
    "title": "00C64YY0C000J",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YY0C000Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YY0C000Z",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Additional context**\nTraced the actual root cause into `src/rust/terminusdb-community/src/doc/mod.rs`. For TerminusDB v12, a single-document `doc get` doesn't go through `json.pl`'s `get_document`/`get_document_` at all — it goes through the Rust foreign predicate `print_document_json`, which calls `DocumentContext::get_id_document`. That's where field-level `@unfold` is actually decided, via a `(parent_type_id, predicate_id)` pair (`unfold_pairs`) read off the *enclosing* stack frame:",
    "title": "00C64YY0C000Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YY0C0010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YY0C0010",
    "children": [],
    "text": "```rust\nlet field_level_unfold = match (parent_type_id, predicate_id) {\n    (Some(pt), Some(pr)) => self.unfold_pairs.contains(&(pt, pr)),\n    _ => false,\n};\n```",
    "title": "00C64YY0C0010",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YY0C0011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YY0C0011",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`parent_type_id`/`predicate_id` come from `StackEntry::document_type_id()` / `current_predicate()`:",
    "title": "00C64YY0C0011",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YY0C0012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YY0C0012",
    "children": [],
    "text": "```rust\nfn document_type_id(&self) -> Option<u64> {\n    match self {\n        Self::Document { type_id, .. } => *type_id,\n        _ => None,\n    }\n}\n\nfn current_predicate(&mut self) -> Option<u64> {\n    match self {\n        Self::Document { fields, .. } => fields.as_mut().and_then(|f| f.peek().map(|t| t.predicate)),\n        _ => None,\n    }\n}\n```",
    "title": "00C64YY0C0012",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YY0C0015",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YY0C0015",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`Set`/`Optional`/`Cardinality` values sit as direct triples on the document, so the enclosing frame is always `StackEntry::Document`, and both lookups succeed — this is why they work.",
    "title": "00C64YY0C0015",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YY0C0017",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YY0C0017",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`List` and `Array` values instead sit behind an intermediate `StackEntry::List` / `StackEntry::Array` frame (the RDF Cons chain for `List`, the array's index triples for `Array`), pushed onto the traversal stack while the document's field is walked. Neither variant carries the parent document's type/predicate forward, so `document_type_id()`/`current_predicate()` fall through the wildcard arm above and return `None` for anything nested inside a `List` or `Array` — silently disabling field-level `@unfold` for their elements regardless of what the schema says.",
    "title": "00C64YY0C0017",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YY0C0019",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YY0C0019",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Class-level `@unfoldable` keeps working regardless, because it's checked via `self.unfoldables.contains(&t.object)`, which only needs the target's own type, not the enclosing frame.",
    "title": "00C64YY0C0019",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64YY0C001D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64YY0C001D",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Fix**\nCapture the parent's `type_id`/`predicate_id` before pushing the `List`/`Array` frame and thread it through `StackEntry::List` and `ArrayStackEntry` so nested elements still match against `unfold_pairs`. Verified against a from-source build: the fix compiles, all existing Rust unit tests still pass, `cargo clippy` shows no new warnings, and new integration tests for `List` retrieval and a 2D `Array` case (added to `tests/test/field-level-unfold.js`, per the request in [the maintainer reply](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/issues/2512#issuecomment-5463157961)) pass against a live server built from the fix.",
    "title": "00C64YY0C001D",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64Z9NB8008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64Z9NB8008",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C64Z9NB8008",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64Z9NB8009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64Z9NB8009",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Artifact Projection** — **implemented and live-verified** (`Aperas-artifact-projection-design.md`, `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A mode 1 of 3). New `web/src/lib/project.ts` serializes an `ArtifactNode`'s ingested `BlockNode` tree back into Markdown — canonical regeneration, not byte-exact: headings/paragraphs/code round-trip via their raw `title`/`text`, while `list`/`blockquote` are regenerated as clean Markdown (list items always blank-line-separated regardless of original tight/loose style; blockquote `> ` re-prefixed uniformly rather than trusting what the raw text happens to carry). Needed two new captured fields on `BlockNode` — `ordered`/`start` (list numbering) and `checked` (task-list checkbox) — both `Optional`, so unlike the `artifactId`/`type` migration this needed no DB wipe. Exposed via `npm run kg:project -- <path>`. Verified two ways: `verifyPhase0.ts`'s synthetic sample (every branch: ordered/unordered/task lists, nested list, blockquote, code) round-trips with zero drift by reusing `reconcile.ts`'s own matcher as the equivalence check; and, more usefully, running the same check against all 11 real artifacts in `AperasKG/artifacts/` surfaced two genuine bugs no synthetic sample had exercised — a nested code fence's closing `` ``` `` desyncing from its opening one because only a multi-line leaf's *first* line has container-required indentation stripped (every later line keeps its literal original-file column; fixed with a `dedent` normalization), and an *indented* (4-space) code block silently degrading into an ordinary paragraph because its raw slice carries no fence to re-emit (fixed by re-fencing it). After those two fixes, 9 of 11 real artifacts round-trip with zero drift; the other 2 each show one accepted, disclosed difference (absolute code-indentation depth normalized to the minimum; indented-style converted to fenced-style) — real content changes under exact-key matching, not bugs, per the design doc §8.",
    "title": "00C64Z9NB8009",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C64Z9NB800G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C64Z9NB800G",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Projection Engines (Phase 1)**: three modes per `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A. The Human UI (SolidJS read-projection, 'Hover Preview' link mechanism, `unfolded` view-state) and the Agentic BFS interface remain undesigned.",
    "title": "00C64Z9NB800G",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:52:24.448Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7G20006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7G20006",
    "children": [],
    "text": "| Module | Location | Responsibilities & Protocol Constraints |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Client Manager** | `web/src/lib/client.ts` | Configures `TerminusDB.WOQLClient`, creates `aperas` database, applies idempotent schema initialization via `full_replace` from `schema.json`. |\n| **Node Identity** | `web/src/lib/snowflake.ts` | Generates the 64-bit Snowflake-style `blockId` (timestamp + machine identity + sequence, 13-char Crockford Base32) — see `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §1.A. |\n| **AST Transducer** | `web/src/lib/astParser.ts` | Uses `unified.js` (`remark-parse`) to recursively convert Markdown into a nested `BlockNode` tree, assigning each block a fresh Snowflake id via `snowflake.ts` (identity is never derived from content or position). |\n| **Artifact Tracking & Ingestion** | `web/src/lib/artifacts.ts` | Computes `fileHash`, tracks/upserts lightweight `ArtifactNode`s, and ingests the full `BlockNode` tree on change — see §4 below. |\n| **Folder Ingestion** | `web/src/lib/folders.ts` | Walks `AperasKG/artifacts/` and commits the `FolderNode` structural tree — see §4 below. |\n| **Extrinsic Assertion CRUD** | `web/src/lib/crud.ts` | `insertAssertion`/`deleteAssertionsInvolvingNode` for the concrete `Assertion` type (source/predicate/target, node-typed per §3 of the design doc's rejection of the `xsd:string` anti-pattern), plus generic best-effort document delete helpers. |\n| **WOQL Engine** | `web/src/lib/woql.ts` | Builds and executes WOQL queries over `Assertion` — `queryNodeAssertions` (both directions) and `traceImpactPropagation` (one-hop sweep along a predicate like `impacts`). |\n| **GraphQL Client** | `web/src/lib/graphql.ts` | `getArtifactTreeViaGraphQL` fetches an `ArtifactNode` and its full nested `BlockNode` tree (bounded depth) via the auto-generated GraphQL endpoint — see the note below on why this is the *only* full-tree read path. |\n| **Version Control** | `web/src/lib/versionControl.ts` | Manages branches, temporal commit histories, version diffing, merge applications (`apply`), and state resets. Class-agnostic — unaffected by the schema redesign. |\n| **JSON-LD Import/Export** | `web/src/lib/export.ts` | `exportJsonLd`/`importJsonLd` mirror the current schema plus every instance document (per class) between TerminusDB and plain JSON-LD files in `AperasKG/Apeiron/` — see §5. |",
    "title": "00C66H7G20006",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7G2001W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7G2001W",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Bridge Addressing**: Remote URLs target the host's Docker bridge gateway (`http://172.17.0.1:6364/admin/aperas`).",
    "title": "00C66H7G2001W",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G000",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G001"
    ],
    "title": "Document Root",
    "type": "root",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G001",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G002",
      "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00A",
      "BlockNode/00C66ZPRQG00C",
      "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00K",
      "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000M",
      "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000P"
    ],
    "title": "# Basic Assertion Authoring — Design",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G002",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G003",
      "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G004",
      "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G009"
    ],
    "title": "## 0. Why this moved out of Phase 2",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G003",
    "children": [],
    "text": "The original roadmap (`Aperas-design.md`'s Development Roadmap) only ever named one assertion-\nrelated deliverable — Phase 2's \"graph search skill\" — and treated the Human UI (Phase 1) as\nthe thing that lets humans/agents see what's in the graph at all. Neither assumption survived\nPhase 0 finishing:",
    "title": "00C67F1KB8003",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G004",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G005",
      "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G007"
    ],
    "ordered": false,
    "start": 1,
    "title": "00C67F1KB8004",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G005",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G006"
    ],
    "title": "00C67F1KB8005",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G006",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`Assertion` (the concrete `BaseEdge`, `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2.C-D) has been a\n  fully working CRUD/WOQL primitive since Phase 0 — `insertAssertion`/`deleteAssertionsInvolvingNode`\n  (`crud.ts`), `queryNodeAssertions`/`traceImpactPropagation` (`woql.ts`) — but the *only* thing\n  that has ever called `insertAssertion` is a single hardcoded synthetic edge in\n  `verifyPhase0.ts`'s test harness. Nothing lets a human or agent write a real one.",
    "title": "00C67F1KB8006",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G007",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G008"
    ],
    "title": "00C67F1KB8007",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G008",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Artifact Projection (`Aperas-artifact-projection-design.md`) turned out to cover the\n  monitoring need the Human UI was assumed to be required for — `kg:project` already lets a\n  human read back exactly what's stored, without a SolidJS front end.",
    "title": "00C67F1KB8008",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G009",
    "children": [],
    "text": "So the basic ability to *write and inspect* a real `Assertion` — with no automated traversal,\nimpact-sweeping, or agent behavior attached — is built now, in Phase 0, so that Phase 1's\nskill-building work starts with it already available rather than blocked on it. Phase 2's\n\"graph search skill\" (`Aperas-design.md` §Development Roadmap, Phase 2) still owns everything\n*automated*: agents that traverse `affects`/`impacts` edges on their own initiative and flag\nstaleness. This doc only covers the manual primitive those agents would eventually build on.",
    "title": "00C67F1KB8009",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00A",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00B"
    ],
    "title": "## 1. Scope: manual, not automated",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00B",
    "children": [],
    "text": "This is a CLI-operated capability, not a skill that runs on its own. A human or an agent\nexplicitly invokes a command to assert one claim, or to look up what's already asserted about\na node. No code here decides *when* to assert something or walks the graph proactively — that\nremains Phase 2 territory, undesigned.",
    "title": "00C67F1KB800B",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00C",
    "children": [],
    "title": "## 2. Two addressing situations, not one \"resolution\" step",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T04:23:56.254Z",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00D",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C66Q1HKG00D",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T04:23:56.254Z",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00E",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C66Q1HKG00E",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T04:23:56.254Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00F",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Direct addressing** — the normal case. You already hold a real node id, from wherever (a\n  prior command's own printed confirmation, a query result, code that still has the parsed\n  tree in memory). `AssertionInput.source`/`target` (`BlockNode/<id>`, `ArtifactNode/<id>`,\n  `FolderNode/<id>`) is passed through completely unchanged — no lookup, no search, nothing to\n  resolve.",
    "title": "00C66Q1HKG00F",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T04:23:56.254Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00G",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C66Q1HKG00G",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T04:23:56.254Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00H",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Addressing from memory** — applies only when there's no id in hand at all, only what a\n  human actually carries in their head. The only node kind with a durable, human-memorable\n  handle is an artifact: people navigate `AperasKG/artifacts/` by file path, never by opaque\n  Snowflake id. So a bare artifact path (e.g. `Aperas-design.md`) is the from-memory form,\n  resolved via the existing `getArtifactRecord` (`artifacts.ts`) to `ArtifactNode/<artifactId>`.\n  Nothing else gets this treatment — `FolderNode` claims are rare enough to just use the raw id.",
    "title": "00C66J7X3000A",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T04:05:00.750Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00J",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`BlockNode` has no from-memory case at all: nobody carries an opaque id in their head, and\nblock content is too long/variable to be a typed-from-memory handle either. So a block-level\nassertion is always direct addressing — its id has to already be in hand from whatever produced\nthe claim (e.g. a skill that just parsed content and still holds the tree). Nothing in Phase 0\ncreates a situation where a human needs to go hunting for a block id with no other context\nalready available, so a \"browse the whole tree to go find and remember an id\" command is\ndeliberately **not** part of this design — it would be solving a problem that doesn't exist yet.\nAdd one later if and when a real workflow actually needs block-granularity claims from a human\nwith no other path to the id.",
    "title": "00C66Q1HKG00J",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T04:23:56.254Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00K",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00M"
    ],
    "title": "## 3. Commands (`kgCli.ts`)",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00M",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00N",
      "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00Q",
      "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00S"
    ],
    "ordered": false,
    "start": 1,
    "title": "00C67F1KB800M",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00N",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66ZPRQG00P"
    ],
    "title": "00C67F1KB800N",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00P",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`kg:assert <source> <predicate> <target>` — `source`/`target` each accept a full node id\n  (direct addressing) or a bare artifact path (from-memory addressing, per §2); calls\n  `insertAssertion`.",
    "title": "00C66Q1HKG00P",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T04:23:56.254Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00Q",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00R"
    ],
    "title": "00C67F1KB800Q",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00R",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`kg:assertions <node>` — same addressing as above, calls `queryNodeAssertions`, prints each\n  as `<direction>  <predicate>  <otherNodeId>`.",
    "title": "00C67F1KB800R",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00S",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00T"
    ],
    "title": "00C67F1KB800S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00T",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`kg:unassert <source> <predicate> <target>` — same addressing, deletes exactly the matching\n  assertion(s), not every assertion touching a node. `deleteAssertionsInvolvingNode` (existing,\n  `crud.ts`) is intentionally blunt — it exists for demo/reset cleanup, where wiping everything\n  touching one node is correct. A real \"undo this claim\" command needs a narrower\n  `deleteAssertion(client, source, predicate, target)`: filter `Assertion` docs on all three\n  fields (not just one), delete only those. New function, `crud.ts`.",
    "title": "00C67F1KB800T",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00V",
    "children": [],
    "title": "## 4. Out of scope",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:54:31.436Z",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00W",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C66H7K7G00W",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:54:31.436Z",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00X",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C66H7K7G00X",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:54:31.436Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00Y",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Any automated creation or traversal (Phase 2's graph search skill).",
    "title": "00C66H7K7G00Y",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:54:31.436Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G00Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G00Z",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C66H7K7G00Z",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:54:31.436Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G010",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Per-asserter branch placement — `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md`'s \"Author-Based Placement\"\n  (§ Design Rationale) describes assertions eventually living in the asserting agent's own\n  branch/artifact so they don't require mutating someone else's. That's a multi-agent-branching\n  concern (Phase 3 territory); today, same as every other write in this codebase, an `Assertion`\n  simply commits to whatever branch the client is already pointed at (`main`). Not addressed\n  here.",
    "title": "00C66H7K7G010",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:54:31.436Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G011",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C66H7K7G011",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:54:31.436Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7K7G012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7K7G012",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Restricting `predicate` to a fixed vocabulary — stays free text, same as `insertAssertion`\n  today; the known examples (`impacts`/`verifies`/`derived_from`/`affects`) remain\n  conventions, not an enum.",
    "title": "00C66H7K7G012",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T03:54:31.436Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NR801C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NR801C",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Fluid Core Substrate: **Apeiron** – The unconditioned, schema-free continuum where thoughts and semantic links flow without artificial boundaries.",
    "title": "00C6HQACHR054",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NR801E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NR801E",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Boundary Projections / Interfaces: **Peras** – The transient, typed structures (JSON Schema, Zod/Pydantic models, AST trees, DB mutations).",
    "title": "00C6HQACHR056",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NRG00S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NRG00S",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NRG00T",
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NRG00V"
    ],
    "text": "Basic assertion authoring (moved earlier from Phase 2, see `Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md`)",
    "title": "00C6HQACJ0031",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NRG00T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NRG00T",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Basic assertion authoring (moved earlier from Phase 2, see `Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md`)",
    "title": "00C6HQACJ0032",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NRG00V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NRG00V",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NRG00W",
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NRG00Y"
    ],
    "ordered": false,
    "start": 1,
    "title": "00C6HQACJ0033",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NRG00W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NRG00W",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NRG00X"
    ],
    "text": "`Assertion` (the concrete `BaseEdge`) was already a working CRUD/WOQL primitive; this adds manual CLI commands (`kg:assert`/`kg:assertions`/`kg:unassert`) so a human or agent can actually write and inspect a real one, with no automated traversal or agent behavior attached.",
    "title": "00C6HQACJ0034",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NRG00X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NRG00X",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`Assertion` (the concrete `BaseEdge`) was already a working CRUD/WOQL primitive; this adds manual CLI commands (`kg:assert`/`kg:assertions`/`kg:unassert`) so a human or agent can actually write and inspect a real one, with no automated traversal or agent behavior attached.",
    "title": "00C6HQACJ0035",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NRG00Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NRG00Y",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NRG00Z"
    ],
    "text": "Built here rather than waiting for Phase 1, so Phase 1's skill-building work starts with it already available instead of blocked on it — and ahead of the SolidJS UI, since Artifact Projection (mode 1 of §5.A) already covers the monitoring need the UI was originally assumed to be required for.",
    "title": "00C6HQACJ0036",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NRG00Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NRG00Z",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Built here rather than waiting for Phase 1, so Phase 1's skill-building work starts with it already available instead of blocked on it — and ahead of the SolidJS UI, since Artifact Projection (mode 1 of §5.A) already covers the monitoring need the UI was originally assumed to be required for.",
    "title": "00C6HQACJ0037",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NRG01P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NRG01P",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Develops specialized procedural skills including ingestion (parsing external text into AST nodes), *automated* graph search (structural traversals and WOQL/GraphQL pattern matching, with vector support evaluated later — the manual assertion primitive itself moved to Phase 1, above), and refactoring (lazy atomization of spans and node merging).",
    "title": "00C6HQACJ004A",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NS004A",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004B",
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004P",
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004Z",
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NS005V",
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NS0066"
    ],
    "ordered": false,
    "start": 1,
    "title": "00C6HQACJ805F",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NS004B",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004C",
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004D"
    ],
    "text": "Historical precedent and human wetware substrate",
    "title": "00C6HQACJ805G",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NS004C",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Historical precedent and human wetware substrate",
    "title": "00C6HQACJ805H",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NS004D",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004E",
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004G",
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004J",
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004M"
    ],
    "ordered": false,
    "start": 1,
    "title": "00C6HQACJ805J",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NS004E",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004F"
    ],
    "text": "Multi-faceted reference model: Inspired by long-standing collaborative systems like Wikipedia (Article, Talk, History, Source), human knowledge work has always relied on multi-perspective nodes rather than monolithic text blobs.",
    "title": "00C6HQACJ805K",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NS004F",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Multi-faceted reference model: Inspired by long-standing collaborative systems like Wikipedia (Article, Talk, History, Source), human knowledge work has always relied on multi-perspective nodes rather than monolithic text blobs.",
    "title": "00C6HQACJ805M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NS004G",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004H"
    ],
    "text": "Software engineering and science operate as a distributed graph running across human mental models and neural networks.",
    "title": "00C6HQACJ805N",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NS004H",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Software engineering and science operate as a distributed graph running across human mental models and neural networks.",
    "title": "00C6HQACJ805P",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NS004J",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004K"
    ],
    "text": "Artifacts like source code, architecture diagrams, and tickets are lossy projections rendered from an underlying mental graph.",
    "title": "00C6HQACJ805Q",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NS004K",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Artifacts like source code, architecture diagrams, and tickets are lossy projections rendered from an underlying mental graph.",
    "title": "00C6HQACJ805R",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H7NS004M",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004N"
    ],
    "text": "Deep reads historically relied on manual navigation, while deep writes relied on mental impact tracing across dependencies.",
    "title": "00C6HQACJ805S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H7NS004N",
