  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H8X0000H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H8X0000H",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Decisive isolation, no second machine needed**: ran two independent TerminusDB containers (`terminusdb` and a throwaway `terminusdb-freshtest`, same image, same physical host, plugin off on both) and bundled/unbundled between them. Same failure. A positive control — unbundling the same file back into a *different database within its own originating container* — succeeded every time. This isolates the variable precisely: **it's not host A vs. host B, or even separate machines at all — it's any two independent TerminusDB stores (server instances/storage directories), even side-by-side on one host.**",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER01M",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H8X0000J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H8X0000J",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Decisive isolation, no second machine needed**: ran two independent TerminusDB containers (`terminusdb` and a throwaway `terminusdb-freshtest`, same image, same physical host, plugin off on both) and bundled/unbundled between them. Same failure. A positive control — unbundling the same file back into a *different database within its own originating container* — succeeded every time. This isolates the variable precisely: **it's not host A vs. host B, or even separate machines at all — it's any two independent TerminusDB stores (server instances/storage directories), even side-by-side on one host.**",
    "title": "00C6834Z7001Y",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:23.296Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H8X0000K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H8X0000K",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Cross-checked against prior art: [terminusdb/terminusdb#1147](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/issues/1147) reports the identical symptom moving a bundle between two instances, never reproduced by the maintainer, closed stale/not-planned. [terminusdb/terminusdb#1235](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/pull/1235) (\"an initial effort to figure out #1147 without success\") added CLI bundle/unbundle tests that call `store.init()` exactly once and share that one store across every test — so its test suite never actually exercises the cross-store case, which is the one that fails.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER01N",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H8X0000M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H8X0000M",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Cross-checked against prior art: [terminusdb/terminusdb#1147](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/issues/1147) reports the identical symptom moving a bundle between two instances, never reproduced by the maintainer, closed stale/not-planned. [terminusdb/terminusdb#1235](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/pull/1235) (\"an initial effort to figure out #1147 without success\") added CLI bundle/unbundle tests that call `store.init()` exactly once and share that one store across every test — so its test suite never actually exercises the cross-store case, which is the one that fails.",
    "title": "00C6834Z70020",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:23.296Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H8X0000N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H8X0000N",
    "children": [],
    "text": "The official [\"move a database between servers\" backup/restore guide](https://terminusdb.org/docs/enterprise-backup-restore/) is scoped to **Enterprise edition** (different mechanism: `/api/bundle` + `/api/unbundle` REST endpoints, target pre-created) — not a documented guarantee for the Community-edition CLI commands we're using.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER01P",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H8X0000P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H8X0000P",
    "children": [],
    "text": "The official [\"move a database between servers\" backup/restore guide](https://terminusdb.org/docs/enterprise-backup-restore/) is scoped to **Enterprise edition** (different mechanism: `/api/bundle` + `/api/unbundle` REST endpoints, target pre-created) — not a documented guarantee for the Community-edition CLI commands we're using.",
    "title": "00C6834Z70022",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:23.296Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H8X0000Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H8X0000Q",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Filed as [terminusdb/terminusdb#2509](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/issues/2509), including the two-store repro and a note about the test-coverage gap in #1235. Draft/record kept at `terminusdb-issue-bundle-cross-store.md`.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER01Q",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H8X0000R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H8X0000R",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Filed as [terminusdb/terminusdb#2509](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/issues/2509), including the two-store repro and a note about the test-coverage gap in #1235. Draft/record kept at `terminusdb-issue-bundle-cross-store.md`.",
    "title": "00C6834Z70024",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:23.296Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H8X0000S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H8X0000S",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Conclusion for this project: `bundle`/`unbundle`/`restore.sh` remain valid for same-store snapshot/rollback, but are not to be relied on for cross-machine transfer.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER01R",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66H8X0000T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66H8X0000T",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Conclusion for this project: `bundle`/`unbundle`/`restore.sh` remain valid for same-store snapshot/rollback, but are not to be relied on for cross-machine transfer.",
    "title": "00C6834Z70026",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:23.296Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66J7X3000M",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000N"
    ],
    "title": "## 4. Real bug found by live testing (fixed)",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66J7X3000N",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`kg:assertions` initially printed the predicate as `[object Object]`. WOQL binds a node-typed\nfield (`source`/`target` — `BaseNode`) as a plain id string, but a literal-typed field\n(`predicate` — `xsd:string`) as a wrapped `{\"@type\": \"xsd:string\", \"@value\": \"...\"}` object —\nconfirmed live by querying directly. `traceImpactPropagation`'s existing test coverage never\ncaught this because it only checks binding *presence*/*membership* (`AffectedNode`, itself\nnode-typed), never a literal field's actual value. Fixed in `woql.ts`'s `queryNodeAssertions`\nwith a small `unwrapLiteral` helper applied to `predicate` in both directions.",
    "title": "00C67F1KB800W",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66J7X3000P",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000Q"
    ],
    "title": "## 5. Out of scope",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66J7X3000Q",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000R",
      "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000T",
      "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000W"
    ],
    "ordered": false,
    "start": 1,
    "title": "00C67F1KB800Y",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66J7X3000R",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000S"
    ],
    "title": "00C67F1KB800Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66J7X3000S",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Any automated creation or traversal (Phase 2's graph search skill).",
    "title": "00C67F1KB8010",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66J7X3000T",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000V"
    ],
    "title": "00C67F1KB8011",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66J7X3000V",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Per-asserter branch placement — `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md`'s \"Author-Based Placement\"\n  (§ Design Rationale) describes assertions eventually living in the asserting agent's own\n  branch/artifact so they don't require mutating someone else's. That's a multi-agent-branching\n  concern (Phase 3 territory); today, same as every other write in this codebase, an `Assertion`\n  simply commits to whatever branch the client is already pointed at (`main`). Not addressed\n  here.",
    "title": "00C67F1KB8012",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66J7X3000W",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000X"
    ],
    "title": "00C67F1KBG000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66J7X3000X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66J7X3000X",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Restricting `predicate` to a fixed vocabulary — stays free text, same as `insertAssertion`\n  today; the known examples (`impacts`/`verifies`/`derived_from`/`affects`) remain\n  conventions, not an enum.",
    "title": "00C67F1KBG001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66J81HG008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66J81HG008",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Basic Assertion Authoring** — **implemented and live-verified** (`Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md`). New `kg:assert`/`kg:assertions`/`kg:unassert` CLI commands (`kgCli.ts`) let a human or agent actually write and inspect a real `Assertion` — previously the only thing that ever called `insertAssertion` was a hardcoded synthetic edge in `verifyPhase0.ts`'s test harness. `source`/`target` accept either a full node id (direct addressing) or a bare artifact/folder path (query-based addressing, resolved via `getArtifactRecord`/the new `getFolderRecord`, artifact tried first since the two never collide) — block-level assertions stay direct-id-only; closing that gap became its own effort, see \"Agentic Query Tools\" below. New narrow `deleteAssertion` (`crud.ts`) does exact source/predicate/target deletion, distinct from the existing blunt `deleteAssertionsInvolvingNode` (kept for demo/reset cleanup only). Live end-to-end test against two real artifacts (assert → list → unassert → list-empty) surfaced one real bug: WOQL binds a literal-typed field (`predicate`, `xsd:string`) as a wrapped `{\"@type\", \"@value\"}` object rather than a plain string, unlike node-typed `source`/`target` bindings — `kg:assertions` printed `[object Object]` until fixed with an `unwrapLiteral` helper in `woql.ts`'s `queryNodeAssertions`. Never caught by `verifyPhase0.ts`'s existing Assertion test because that only checks binding presence/membership, never a literal field's actual value. Reordered from Phase 2 into Phase 0 itself (not merely Phase 1) per `Aperas-design.md`'s roadmap, so Phase 1 skill-building starts with it already available.",
    "title": "00C6834Z7000X",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:23.296Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66J81HG009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66J81HG009",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Basic Assertion Authoring** — **implemented and live-verified** (`Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md`). New `kg:assert`/`kg:assertions`/`kg:unassert` CLI commands (`kgCli.ts`) let a human or agent actually write and inspect a real `Assertion` — previously the only thing that ever called `insertAssertion` was a hardcoded synthetic edge in `verifyPhase0.ts`'s test harness. `source`/`target` accept either a full node id (direct addressing) or a bare artifact path (addressing from memory, resolved via the existing `getArtifactRecord`) — block-level assertions stay direct-id-only, since nothing in Phase 0 creates a situation where a human needs to hunt for a block id with no other context, so no \"browse the tree\" command was built. New narrow `deleteAssertion` (`crud.ts`) does exact source/predicate/target deletion, distinct from the existing blunt `deleteAssertionsInvolvingNode` (kept for demo/reset cleanup only). Live end-to-end test against two real artifacts (assert → list → unassert → list-empty) surfaced one real bug: WOQL binds a literal-typed field (`predicate`, `xsd:string`) as a wrapped `{\"@type\", \"@value\"}` object rather than a plain string, unlike node-typed `source`/`target` bindings — `kg:assertions` printed `[object Object]` until fixed with an `unwrapLiteral` helper in `woql.ts`'s `queryNodeAssertions`. Never caught by `verifyPhase0.ts`'s existing Assertion test because that only checks binding presence/membership, never a literal field's actual value. Reordered from Phase 2 into Phase 0 itself (not merely Phase 1) per `Aperas-design.md`'s roadmap, so Phase 1 skill-building starts with it already available.",
    "title": "00C66J81HG009",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T04:05:03.495Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66Q1HKG00H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66Q1HKG00H",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Addressing from memory** — applies only when there's no id in hand at all, only what a\n  human actually carries in their head. Both node kinds people navigate by path — not by opaque\n  Snowflake id — get this treatment identically: a bare artifact path (e.g. `Aperas-design.md`)\n  resolves via the existing `getArtifactRecord` (`artifacts.ts`) to `ArtifactNode/<artifactId>`,\n  and a bare folder path (e.g. `.` for the artifacts root, or a subdirectory name) resolves via\n  the new, structurally identical `getFolderRecord` (`folders.ts`) to `FolderNode/<folderId>`.\n  An artifact path and a folder path never collide, so trying artifact-then-folder resolution in\n  that order is unambiguous.",
    "title": "00C66Q1HKG00H",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T04:23:56.254Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66Q1MC803M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C66Q1MC803M",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C5FPAA2006B",
      "BlockNode/00C66Q1MC803P"
    ],
    "text": "Multi-faceted node interface",
    "title": "00C6HQACJ003Y",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C66Q1MC803N",
