    "children": [],
    "text": "Now that §2's fix gives `heading`/`listItem` a real abstract, only a `list` child (the one\n  true content-less container per §2) ever has nothing to show — it prints\n  `<id>  [<kind>]  (no text of its own — see kg:unfold <id>)`. Every other child kind now has\n  something real to display.",
    "title": "00C6833Y6G01E",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6833Y6G01F",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01G"
    ],
    "text": "**Persists**, not just displays: also sets `BlockNode.unfolded = true` on the target node —\n  the first code path to ever write `true` to that field (it's existed since early in the\n  schema, defaulted `false` on creation, carried forward by reconciliation, but never\n  meaningfully written or read — confirmed live by grepping the codebase). Implementation note:\n  `BlockNode.children` is a required `List`, so the write has to resubmit the full existing\n  document with only `unfolded` changed (fetch-then-resubmit), the same pattern\n  `tombstoneSubtree` (`reconcile.ts`) already uses for a different field on the same class.",
    "title": "00C6833Y6G01F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6833Y6G01G",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Persists**, not just displays: also sets `BlockNode.unfolded = true` on the target node —\n  the first code path to ever write `true` to that field (it's existed since early in the\n  schema, defaulted `false` on creation, carried forward by reconciliation, but never\n  meaningfully written or read — confirmed live by grepping the codebase). Implementation note:\n  `BlockNode.children` is a required `List`, so the write has to resubmit the full existing\n  document with only `unfolded` changed (fetch-then-resubmit), the same pattern\n  `tombstoneSubtree` (`reconcile.ts`) already uses for a different field on the same class.",
    "title": "00C6833Y6G01G",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6833Y6G01H",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01J"
    ],
    "text": "`kg:fold <path>` is the exact inverse: sets `unfolded = false` on the target node, no content\n  printed beyond a confirmation — there's nothing new to show by re-collapsing.",
    "title": "00C6833Y6G01H",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6833Y6G01J",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`kg:fold <path>` is the exact inverse: sets `unfolded = false` on the target node, no content\n  printed beyond a confirmation — there's nothing new to show by re-collapsing.",
    "title": "00C6833Y6G01J",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6833Y6G01K",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Composability, corrected**: an earlier revision described `kg:tree` + `kg:unfold` as\n\"composable\" but only demonstrated a *sequence* of separate calls (`tree` → `unfold x` →\n`unfold y` → ...) whose results a reader has to mentally combine — not a single artifact. What\n\"composable\" should actually mean: the *persisted* `unfolded` state left behind by a sequence of\n`kg:fold`/`kg:unfold` calls is a standing, queryable shape — \"a selectively expanded tree\" — not\njust this session's scrollback. This design doesn't yet specify a single command that renders\nthat combined shape in one shot (e.g. a `kg:tree` variant that shows full text at nodes flagged\n`unfolded` and stops at folded ones, rather than uniformly title-only) — that's a natural next\nstep once `kg:fold`/`kg:unfold` exist and something has actually been unfolded, but it's a real\ndesign fork (should `kg:tree` change its default behavior, or should this be a separate command)\nworth settling deliberately rather than folding in by assumption here.",
    "title": "00C6833Y6G01K",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6833Y6G01M",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01N",
      "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01P",
      "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01Q"
    ],
    "text": "Command: `kg:search <pattern>` — PCRE-style regex, matching `WOQL.re`'s own semantics.",
    "title": "## 5. `kg:search` — keyword/regex content search",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6833Y6G01N",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Command: `kg:search <pattern>` — PCRE-style regex, matching `WOQL.re`'s own semantics.",
    "title": "00C6833Y6G01N",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6833Y6G01P",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Confirmed live (not just documented): TerminusDB's WOQL builder exposes\n`WOQL.re(pattern, inputVarName, resultVarList)` (`terminusdb` npm package, `lib/woql.js:1262`)\n— genuine server-side regex matching, not a client-side scan. Tested directly against the real\nKG: `WOQL.re('Assertion', titleVar, matchVar)` combined with `WOQL.triple(doc, 'title',\ntitleVar)` correctly matched 3 real `BlockNode` titles containing \"Assertion.\" A pattern with a\nregex metacharacter (`kg:\\w+`) also matched correctly using ordinary single-level JS string\nescaping — the \"escape twice\" note in the client's own docstring turned out to be about\ninternal JSON-LD wire transport, already handled by the client library; callers don't need to\ndouble-escape anything themselves.",
    "title": "00C6833Y6G01P",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6833Y6G01Q",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01R",
      "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01T",
      "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01W"
    ],
    "ordered": false,
    "start": 1,
    "title": "00C6833Y6G01Q",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6833Y6G01R",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01S"
    ],
    "text": "Searches `title` and `text` across all three node classes (`BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode`,\n  `FolderNode`) — six queries (3 classes × 2 fields), each\n  `WOQL.and(WOQL.triple(v.Doc, field, v.Value), WOQL.re(pattern, v.Value, v.Match))`. A\n  class/field a given document doesn't have (e.g. `Optional text` absent) simply produces no\n  triple to match against — no special-casing needed.",
    "title": "00C6833Y6G01R",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6833Y6G01S",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Searches `title` and `text` across all three node classes (`BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode`,\n  `FolderNode`) — six queries (3 classes × 2 fields), each\n  `WOQL.and(WOQL.triple(v.Doc, field, v.Value), WOQL.re(pattern, v.Value, v.Match))`. A\n  class/field a given document doesn't have (e.g. `Optional text` absent) simply produces no\n  triple to match against — no special-casing needed.",
    "title": "00C6833Y6G01S",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6833Y6G01T",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01V"
    ],
    "text": "`title`/`text` are `xsd:string` literals, so bindings come back wrapped\n  (`{\"@type\", \"@value\"}`), exactly like `predicate` did in `Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md`\n  §4 — reuses the existing `unwrapLiteral` (`woql.ts`) rather than re-solving the same problem.",
    "title": "00C6833Y6G01T",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6833Y6G01V",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`title`/`text` are `xsd:string` literals, so bindings come back wrapped\n  (`{\"@type\", \"@value\"}`), exactly like `predicate` did in `Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md`\n  §4 — reuses the existing `unwrapLiteral` (`woql.ts`) rather than re-solving the same problem.",
    "title": "00C6833Y6G01V",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6833Y6G01W",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01X"
    ],
    "text": "Output: `<id>  [<kind>]  <field>  <value>` per match, across all six queries, deduplicated by\n  id (a node could match on both `title` and `text`).",
    "title": "00C6833Y6G01W",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6833Y6G01X",
