    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R006",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C6MMB190006",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R007",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`title`**: this node's own folded label. Meaningful today only where the source actually\n  supplies one (a `heading`'s literal heading line); everywhere else it's a `blockId` fallback,\n  standing in for \"nothing has summarized me yet\" (AI-driven title summarization is a separate,\n  unbuilt enhancement — `Aperas-dev-status.md`, \"Fractal Ontology Enhancements\"). That fallback\n  is itself meaningful signal (`Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md` §2), not a gap to hide.",
    "title": "00C6MMB190007",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R008",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`title`**: this node's own folded label. Meaningful today only where the source actually\n  supplies one (a `heading`'s literal heading line); everywhere else it's a `blockId` fallback,\n  standing in for \"nothing has summarized me yet\" (AI-driven title summarization is a separate,\n  unbuilt enhancement — `Aperas-dev-status.md`, \"Fractal Ontology Enhancements\"). That fallback\n  is itself meaningful signal (`Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md` §2), not a gap to hide.",
    "title": "00C6MMB190008",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R009",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`text`**: this node's own *immediate* content — not a cache, not a derived read-only summary\n  computed from elsewhere and bolted on after the fact. If a node has a `text`, that content is\n  *this node's*, the same way a `paragraph`'s `text` unquestionably belongs to it and to no\n  other node.",
    "title": "00C6MMB190009",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00A",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`text`**: this node's own *immediate* content — not a cache, not a derived read-only summary\n  computed from elsewhere and bolted on after the fact. If a node has a `text`, that content is\n  *this node's*, the same way a `paragraph`'s `text` unquestionably belongs to it and to no\n  other node.",
    "title": "00C6MMB19000A",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00B",
    "children": [],
    "text": "That second point is the one worth stating plainly, because the code shipped earlier this\nsession violated it: `heading`/`listItem`'s `text` was computed via `extractAbstract(block)` —\na **copy** of whatever descendant paragraph happened to be found first, deep search included,\nwhile that same paragraph *also* remained, fully duplicated, as its own independent child\n`BlockNode`. That's inconsistent with every leaf type, where `text` is never a duplicate of\nsomething addressable elsewhere. §2 below supersedes that with a consuming (not copying) rule\nfor `heading`/`listItem` specifically, which is what makes `text` mean the same thing — *my own\ncontent, not a copy of someone else's* — across every node type uniformly.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G003",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00C",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00E",
      "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00F",
      "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R00A"
    ],
    "text": "**Settled**: `heading` and `listItem` are structurally equal — both are \"the main block type\": a\nlabel (title, or its blockId-fallback equivalent) plus optional own content (`text`) plus\nstructural children. A `heading` is not special-cased relative to a `listItem`; the only\ndifference is that a heading's `title` happens to already be meaningful (the heading line) while\na listItem's isn't (no natural label).",
    "title": "## 2. `heading` and `listItem`: equal, and *consume* their leading paragraph",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00D",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Settled**: `heading` and `listItem` are structurally equal — both are \"the main block type\": a\nlabel (title, or its blockId-fallback equivalent) plus optional own content (`text`) plus\nstructural children. A `heading` is not special-cased relative to a `listItem`; the only\ndifference is that a heading's `title` happens to already be meaningful (the heading line) while\na listItem's isn't (no natural label).",
    "title": "00C6MMB19000D",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00E",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Consuming, not copying**: when a `heading` or `listItem` node's *immediate* first child (after\n`groupByHeadings`, for headings) is a `paragraph`, that paragraph's `text` becomes this node's\nown `text`, and the paragraph is **not** kept as a separate child — it's absorbed, one node\nfewer in the tree, not two nodes carrying identical content. If the first child isn't a\nparagraph (e.g. a heading immediately followed by a sub-heading, or a listItem containing only a\nnested list with no leading sentence — both legal Markdown), `text` stays empty; there's nothing\nof the node's own to consume, same as today.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G005",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00F",
    "children": [],
    "text": "This is a **shallow** rule — the immediate child only, not a recursive search through nested\ncontainers. That's a deliberate change from the shipped `extractAbstract`-based version, which\ndug arbitrarily deep (through a `list`/`listItem` chain) to find *some* text to copy. Under the\nconsuming model, a heading whose first child is a `list` (not a `paragraph`) simply has no\n`text` of its own — its content genuinely lives only in its children, and that's an accurate\nstatement about the document, not a gap to paper over by reaching deeper for something to show.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G006",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00G",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**What this supersedes**: the `astParser.ts` change made earlier this session (`extractAbstract`\nreused for `heading`/`listItem`, called after `children` exist) is **not** this design — it was\na copy, and a deep one. It should be replaced by the consuming, shallow version described here.\nNot yet done — this doc settles the design; implementation is pending.",
    "title": "00C6MMB19000G",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00H",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Explicitly **not** decided here. Today it falls through to the ordinary leaf default (`text =\nrawText`, the full raw source slice, quote markers included) — carrying real content again\ninstead of being emptied like `list`, which is an improvement over the prior \"container, no\ntext\" treatment, but whether that's actually the right *final* answer is open. Complications\nalready on record work against a quick unification with §2's consuming rule: a blockquote's raw\nslice keeps literal `> ` markers exactly as the source wrote them (stray or not, per the\nlazy-continuation finding in `Aperas-artifact-projection-design.md` §3), which is not the same\nstring as what \"consuming children\" would produce (marker-stripped, as `project.ts`'s\n`serializeBlock` already reconstructs at output time) — so unlike `heading`/`listItem`, a\nblockquote's `text` isn't cleanly \"the same content, just captured earlier.\" Revisit later, once\nthere's a concrete reason to (a real query or projection need pushing on it), rather than forcing\na decision now.",
    "title": "## 3. `blockquote`: deferred",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:58:08.194Z",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00J",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Explicitly **not** decided here. Today it falls through to the ordinary leaf default (`text =\nrawText`, the full raw source slice, quote markers included) — carrying real content again\ninstead of being emptied like `list`, which is an improvement over the prior \"container, no\ntext\" treatment, but whether that's actually the right *final* answer is open. Complications\nalready on record work against a quick unification with §2's consuming rule: a blockquote's raw\nslice keeps literal `> ` markers exactly as the source wrote them (stray or not, per the\nlazy-continuation finding in `Aperas-artifact-projection-design.md` §3), which is not the same\nstring as what \"consuming children\" would produce (marker-stripped, as `project.ts`'s\n`serializeBlock` already reconstructs at output time) — so unlike `heading`/`listItem`, a\nblockquote's `text` isn't cleanly \"the same content, just captured earlier.\" Revisit later, once\nthere's a concrete reason to (a real query or projection need pushing on it), rather than forcing\na decision now.",
    "title": "00C6KX5Z4R00E",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:58:08.194Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00K",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Checked empirically against the actual parser rather than assumed — including a second pass\nafter the first revision of this doc, since two of its follow-up questions (\"have you actually\nchecked gfm's table support\" / \"what about bold and italic\") turned out not to have been\nverified as precisely as they should have been.",
    "title": "## 4. Coverage audit: what else is out there?",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:00:03.507Z",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00M",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Checked empirically against the actual parser (`unified().use(remarkParse)`, **no**\n`remark-gfm`) rather than assumed. Two real, previously-unflagged gaps surfaced:",
    "title": "00C6HQBE0R00M",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T17:41:14.782Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00N",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C6KAEKM800H",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:00:03.507Z",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00P",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`thematicBreak`/`html` — fixed.** Both now in `astParser.ts`'s `isStructural` list, treated\n  as ordinary leaves (`text = rawText`, same as `code`, no special-casing needed since they\n  already fell through to the default). Added to `reconcile.ts`'s `LEAF_TYPES` too — without\n  that, every `thematicBreak`/`html` node would be invisible to both matching stages and show as\n  removed+added on *every* re-ingestion regardless of whether it changed, since a node whose type\n  is in neither `LEAF_TYPES` nor `CONTAINER_TYPES` never gets matched at all. Also added an\n  explicit `project.ts` case (`paragraph`/`thematicBreak`/`html` now share one case) — without\n  it, `serializeBlock`'s `default` case (children-join) would emit nothing for these two, since\n  they have no children, silently dropping them again at projection time even though ingestion\n  now keeps them. Verified: `verify:phase0 -- --db` clean, sample markdown extended to exercise\n  both.",
    "title": "00C6KAEKM800J",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:00:03.507Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00Q",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Task-list `checked` is currently non-functional.** GFM task-list syntax (`- [ ] `/`- [x] `)\n  is a `remark-gfm` extension; this project has never depended on `remark-gfm` (confirmed:\n  absent from `package.json`). Tested directly: parsing `- [ ] task item` / `- [x] done item`\n  with plain `remark-parse` yields `checked: null` for *every* item, regardless of source syntax\n  — the `[ ]`/`[x]` text is left as literal characters inside an ordinary paragraph, never\n  recognized as a checkbox at all. This directly contradicts earlier claims this session that\n  `checked` capture was \"confirmed live\" — what was actually confirmed was that the *round-trip*\n  test passes, which only asserts `added === 0 && removed === 0` (structural equivalence); it\n  never asserts on `checked`'s actual value, so `null` on both the original and re-parsed sides\n  passes silently. A real regression hiding behind a test that wasn't checking what it needed to.\n  **Fix**: add `remark-gfm` as a dependency and `.use(remarkGfm)` to the processor pipeline in\n  `astParser.ts`. Not done here — flagged, not fixed, pending a decision to proceed.",
    "title": "00C6HQBE0R00Q",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T17:41:14.782Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00R",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Task-list `checked` is genuinely non-functional today — confirmed, not yet fixed.** GFM\n  task-list syntax is a `remark-gfm` extension; this project has never depended on it (confirmed\n  absent from `package.json` before this investigation). Tested directly: `- [ ] `/`- [x] ` with\n  plain `remark-parse` yields `checked: null` for every item regardless of source syntax — the\n  brackets are left as literal text, never recognized as a checkbox. This contradicts an earlier\n  claim this session that `checked` capture was \"confirmed live\" — what was actually confirmed\n  was only the round-trip test's `added === 0 && removed === 0`, which passes trivially when\n  both sides are `null`. `ordered`/`start` are unaffected (core CommonMark, confirmed live\n  independently of this).",
    "title": "00C6KAEKM800M",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:00:03.507Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00S",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`ordered`/`start` are fine** — list numbering is core CommonMark, not a GFM extension;\n  confirmed live (`{ordered: true, start: 3}` for a `3. third\\n4. fourth` list) independent of\n  the `checked` problem above.",
    "title": "00C6HQBE0R00S",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T17:41:14.782Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00T",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`remark-gfm` installed and its table support checked directly, per the follow-up question.**\n  Not merely assumed: parsed `| Left | Center | Right |` / `|:---|:---:|---:|` / two data rows\n  with `.use(remarkParse).use(remarkGfm)`. Result: full support — a proper `table` node with\n  `align: [\"left\",\"center\",\"right\"]` (per-column, all three GFM alignment forms recognized) and\n  ordinary `tableRow`/`tableCell` children (the header is just row 0 by convention, no separate\n  \"header\" node type) — arbitrary row/column counts, not just the 2×3 tested. Genuinely complete\n  for this project's purposes, not partial.",
    "title": "00C6KAEKM800P",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:00:03.507Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00V",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`thematicBreak` (`---`) and `html` (raw HTML blocks) are silently dropped, losing content.**\n  Both parse into their own distinct mdast node types (confirmed live), but neither appears in\n  `astParser.ts`'s `isStructural` list (`['root', 'paragraph', 'heading', 'listItem', 'code',\n  'blockquote', 'list']`) — `convertAstNode` returns `null` for both, and the parent simply\n  omits them from `children`. For a container whose own `text` is intentionally empty (`root`,\n  `list`, and — pre-§2 — `heading`), this isn't \"absorbed into the parent's text\" the way an\n  unrecognized *inline* element would be; it's outright **data loss**, silent, no warning logged.\n  A horizontal rule or an embedded raw-HTML block in a real artifact today just vanishes from the\n  KG on ingestion. Not fixed here — needs its own decision (most likely: treat both as a new leaf\n  type, `text = rawText`, no children, same shape as `code`).",
    "title": "00C6HQBE0R00V",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T17:41:14.782Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00W",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`table` — decided (opaque) and implemented.** `remark-gfm` now wired into `astParser.ts`'s\n  real pipeline (`.use(remarkParse).use(remarkGfm)`), which also genuinely fixes `checked` as a\n  coupled side effect (confirmed: `verifyPhase0.ts` now asserts on the actual `checked` value,\n  not just round-trip shape, and passes). `table` added to `isStructural` as one more opaque\n  leaf (`text = rawText`), same treatment as `code`/`thematicBreak`/`html` — no per-row/per-cell\n  decomposition; `table`'s own children come out empty for free, since `tableRow`/`tableCell`\n  aren't structural either, so nothing needs excluding explicitly. Added to `reconcile.ts`'s\n  `LEAF_TYPES` and `project.ts`'s leaf-passthrough case, same as the `thematicBreak`/`html` fix.\n  Verified: `verify:phase0 -- --db` clean, sample markdown extended with a real aligned table.\n  **Migration note, reasoned not yet forced**: three real artifacts (`Aperas-architecture.md`,\n  `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md`, `tdb-cli-status-walkthrough.md`) already contain real tables,\n  currently stored as opaque `paragraph`s from before this change — `kg:track`'s hash-gate means\n  they won't re-ingest until touched (same limitation noted in §4's `thematicBreak`/`html` entry\n  originally). When they do, the type change (`paragraph` → `table`) should reconcile cleanly\n  rather than showing as removed+added: `reconcile.ts`'s Stage A matches purely on `leafKey`'s\n  *string value* (`node.text`), never comparing `.type` between old and new — so a `paragraph`\n  and a `table` with byte-identical `text` (the same table, same raw slice, only the recognized\n  type differs) match as the same node, and `blockId` carries over. Reasoned from re-reading\n  `diffChildren`, not forced against real content this session.",
    "title": "00C6KAEKM800R",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:00:03.507Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00X",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Tables degrade to opaque text, but don't lose data.** Without `remark-gfm`, a Markdown table\n  doesn't parse as `table`/`tableRow`/`tableCell` at all — confirmed live: `| A | B |\\n|---|---|\\n\n  | 1 | 2 |` parses as a single ordinary `paragraph` whose `text` is the entire raw table markup,\n  verbatim. Unlike `thematicBreak`/`html`, this isn't silent data loss — the content survives\n  (and would round-trip correctly through Artifact Projection, since a `paragraph`'s `text` is\n  replayed as-is) — but it's structurally opaque: no per-row/per-cell addressability, no query\n  visibility into table content as a table. A real limitation, not a bug — the same category as\n  \"no AI summarization yet,\" a capability not yet built rather than something broken.",
    "title": "00C6HQBE0R00X",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T17:41:14.782Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00Y",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Wikilinks (`[[Page]]`, `[[Page|Alias]]`) — confirmed unrecognized, same non-loss-but-opaque\n  category as everything else inline.** Tested directly: plain `remark-parse` (with or without\n  `remark-gfm`) doesn't recognize wikilink syntax at all — no plugin for it is installed, and\n  nothing in CommonMark/GFM defines it. `[[Some Page]]` parses as one plain `text` node, brackets\n  included, verbatim — not even partially mis-parsed as an ordinary link. Contrast: an *ordinary*\n  `[text](url)` link **is** already recognized as a proper `link` mdast node by plain\n  `remark-parse` (core CommonMark, confirmed live) — but nothing extracts it either (the\n  pre-existing, already-tracked \"Fractal Ontology Enhancements\" gap). Wikilinks would need\n  their own plugin (e.g. `remark-wiki-link`) even to reach that same starting point. Per this\n  turn's decision on inline features generally: left as-is, not in scope for this doc.",
    "title": "00C6KAEKM800T",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:00:03.507Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R00Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R00Z",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Strikethrough (`~~text~~`) and footnotes (`[^1]`)** are inline-level, not block-level —\n  out of scope for this doc's mapping (which is about block/container shape), but noted for\n  completeness: both are also GFM/extension-dependent and currently just literal text, same\n  non-loss-but-opaque category as tables.",
    "title": "00C6HQBE0R00Z",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T17:41:14.782Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R010",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Bold/italic: already fully correct today, independent of `remark-gfm`.** `**bold**`/\n  `*italic*` are core CommonMark — confirmed live (`strong`/`emphasis` mdast nodes) with plain\n  `remark-parse`, no extension needed. More importantly, it wouldn't matter either way: this\n  project never re-serializes inline content — `paragraph.text` (and every other leaf's `text`)\n  is a raw source-position slice, captured and replayed byte-for-byte regardless of whether\n  remark's own AST recognizes an inline span as `strong`/`emphasis`/anything else. The same\n  applies to strikethrough's actual *text* (the tildes survive verbatim in the raw slice whether\n  or not gfm is present) — gfm only changes whether remark's AST additionally recognizes it as a\n  distinct `delete` node internally, which nothing in this codebase reads today (inline-level\n  structural extraction — links, emphasis spans, etc. — remains the separately-tracked, unbuilt\n  \"Fractal Ontology Enhancements\" item). Confirmed live both with and without `remark-gfm` to be\n  sure this claim wasn't just assumed either.",
    "title": "00C6KAEKM800W",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:00:03.507Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R011",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**YAML frontmatter** (`---\\nkey: value\\n---` at a file's start) isn't specifically handled\n  either (`remark-frontmatter` not installed) — the opening `---` would parse as a lone\n  `thematicBreak` (see above) and the frontmatter body as ordinary paragraphs. Not tested against\n  a real file this session; flagged as a likely footgun for future artifact authors, not\n  confirmed as an active problem today (no current artifact uses frontmatter).",
    "title": "00C6HQBE0R011",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T17:41:14.782Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R012",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Once §2's consuming rule ships, `project.ts`'s `serializeBlock` **must** change for `heading`/\n`serializeListItem` — today both build their output purely via `renderChildren`/recursion,\nnever reading `node.text` (confirmed by re-reading `project.ts` this session). Under the\n*copying* model that was safe (the paragraph was still there to recurse into); under the\n*consuming* model it would silently **drop the leading sentence from every projected document**,\nsince it no longer exists as a child at all. This is not an optional follow-up — it's required\nfor correctness the moment §2 is implemented, and should ship in the same change, not after.",
    "title": "## 5. Consequences for Artifact Projection (not yet made)",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T17:41:14.782Z",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R013",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R013",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Once §2's consuming rule ships, `project.ts`'s `serializeBlock` **must** change for `heading`/\n`serializeListItem` — today both build their output purely via `renderChildren`/recursion,\nnever reading `node.text` (confirmed by re-reading `project.ts` this session). Under the\n*copying* model that was safe (the paragraph was still there to recurse into); under the\n*consuming* model it would silently **drop the leading sentence from every projected document**,\nsince it no longer exists as a child at all. This is not an optional follow-up — it's required\nfor correctness the moment §2 is implemented, and should ship in the same change, not after.",
    "title": "00C6HQBE0R013",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T17:41:14.782Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R014",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R014",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`reconcile.ts` matches `heading` by `.title` (Stage A) and `listItem` by type+position (Stage B)\n— neither key depends on the *value* of `.text`. So consuming the leading paragraph doesn't\nchange either node's own matching behavior. What it does change: today, that leading paragraph\nis a separate child, itself Stage-A-matched by *exact text equality* — editing just that\nsentence makes it fail to match (declined, shows as remove+add, per the reconciliation design's\n\"decline rather than guess\" principle) even though the heading/listItem around it is unchanged.\nOnce consumed, that same edit becomes an in-place field update on the already-matched\nheading/listItem (no separate node to fail-to-match) — arguably a more natural outcome (\"I\ntweaked the section's opening line\" reads as an edit, not a delete-and-recreate), but a real,\nworth-flagging shift in reconciliation stats for anything that edits leading sentences under\nheadings or list items.",
    "title": "## 6. Consequences for Reconciliation (a positive side effect, worth naming)",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T17:41:14.782Z",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R015",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R015",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`reconcile.ts` matches `heading` by `.title` (Stage A) and `listItem` by type+position (Stage B)\n— neither key depends on the *value* of `.text`. So consuming the leading paragraph doesn't\nchange either node's own matching behavior. What it does change: today, that leading paragraph\nis a separate child, itself Stage-A-matched by *exact text equality* — editing just that\nsentence makes it fail to match (declined, shows as remove+add, per the reconciliation design's\n\"decline rather than guess\" principle) even though the heading/listItem around it is unchanged.\nOnce consumed, that same edit becomes an in-place field update on the already-matched\nheading/listItem (no separate node to fail-to-match) — arguably a more natural outcome (\"I\ntweaked the section's opening line\" reads as an edit, not a delete-and-recreate), but a real,\nworth-flagging shift in reconciliation stats for anything that edits leading sentences under\nheadings or list items.",
    "title": "00C6HQBE0R015",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T17:41:14.782Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R016",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R016",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Decided: §1 (uniform title/text semantics), §2 (heading/listItem consume, shallow). Deferred:\n§3 (blockquote). Flagged, not yet fixed: §4's `remark-gfm` gap (`checked`, tables) and the\n`thematicBreak`/`html` data-loss gap. Required, not yet done: §5 (`project.ts` changes). Not yet\nimplemented anywhere in code — this doc settles the design only.",
    "title": "## 7. Status",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T17:41:14.782Z",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6HQBE0R017",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6HQBE0R017",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Decided: §1 (uniform title/text semantics), §2 (heading/listItem consume, shallow). Deferred:\n§3 (blockquote). Flagged, not yet fixed: §4's `remark-gfm` gap (`checked`, tables) and the\n`thematicBreak`/`html` data-loss gap. Required, not yet done: §5 (`project.ts` changes). Not yet\nimplemented anywhere in code — this doc settles the design only.",
    "title": "00C6HQBE0R017",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T17:41:14.782Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G00K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G00K",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Checked empirically against the actual parser rather than assumed — including a second pass\nafter the first revision of this doc, since two of its follow-up questions (\"have you actually\nchecked gfm's table support\" / \"what about bold and italic\") turned out not to have been\nverified as precisely as they should have been.",
    "title": "00C6KAEKM800G",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:00:03.507Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G00P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G00P",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`thematicBreak`/`html` — fixed.** Both now in `astParser.ts`'s `isStructural` list, treated\n  as ordinary leaves (`text = rawText`, same as `code`, no special-casing needed since they\n  already fell through to the default). Added to `reconcile.ts`'s `LEAF_TYPES` too — without\n  that, every `thematicBreak`/`html` node would be invisible to both matching stages and show as\n  removed+added on *every* re-ingestion regardless of whether it changed, since a node whose type\n  is in neither `LEAF_TYPES` nor `CONTAINER_TYPES` never gets matched at all. Also added an\n  explicit `project.ts` case (`paragraph`/`thematicBreak`/`html` now share one case) — without\n  it, `serializeBlock`'s `default` case (children-join) would emit nothing for these two, since\n  they have no children, silently dropping them again at projection time even though ingestion\n  now keeps them. Verified: `verify:phase0 -- --db` clean, sample markdown extended to exercise\n  both.",
    "title": "00C6KAEKM800K",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:00:03.507Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G00R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G00R",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Task-list `checked` is genuinely non-functional today — confirmed, not yet fixed.** GFM\n  task-list syntax is a `remark-gfm` extension; this project has never depended on it (confirmed\n  absent from `package.json` before this investigation). Tested directly: `- [ ] `/`- [x] ` with\n  plain `remark-parse` yields `checked: null` for every item regardless of source syntax — the\n  brackets are left as literal text, never recognized as a checkbox. This contradicts an earlier\n  claim this session that `checked` capture was \"confirmed live\" — what was actually confirmed\n  was only the round-trip test's `added === 0 && removed === 0`, which passes trivially when\n  both sides are `null`. `ordered`/`start` are unaffected (core CommonMark, confirmed live\n  independently of this).",
    "title": "00C6KAEKM800N",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:00:03.507Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G00T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G00T",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`remark-gfm` installed and its table support checked directly, per the follow-up question.**\n  Not merely assumed: parsed `| Left | Center | Right |` / `|:---|:---:|---:|` / two data rows\n  with `.use(remarkParse).use(remarkGfm)`. Result: full support — a proper `table` node with\n  `align: [\"left\",\"center\",\"right\"]` (per-column, all three GFM alignment forms recognized) and\n  ordinary `tableRow`/`tableCell` children (the header is just row 0 by convention, no separate\n  \"header\" node type) — arbitrary row/column counts, not just the 2×3 tested. Genuinely complete\n  for this project's purposes, not partial.",
    "title": "00C6KAEKM800Q",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:00:03.507Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G00W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G00W",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Wiring `remark-gfm` in for real is coupled to a table decision — not a free fix.** Once\n  `.use(remarkGfm)` is added (to fix `checked`, genuinely), a Markdown table's mdast type changes\n  from an opaque `paragraph` (today) to a real `table` — and `table` isn't in `isStructural`\n  either. Without also deciding how to handle it, this would **regress** table content from\n  \"opaque but preserved\" (today) into \"silently dropped\" (the exact `thematicBreak`/`html` bug,\n  reintroduced for a different type). This isn't hypothetical: checked directly against the real\n  KG (`grep` across `AperasKG/artifacts/`) — `Aperas-architecture.md`, `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md`,\n  and `tdb-cli-status-walkthrough.md` all contain genuine GFM tables today (confirmed one\n  directly: `Aperas-architecture.md`'s class-comparison table, header row plus `:---` alignment).\n  Wiring gfm in without a table plan would lose real content on next ingestion for three live\n  artifacts. Recommended (not yet decided): treat `table` as one more opaque leaf, same treatment\n  as `code`/`thematicBreak`/`html` — `text = rawText`, no per-row/per-cell decomposition — an\n  explicit \"not yet a capability\" choice (matching AI summarization's status) rather than a bug,\n  revisited only if per-cell query/addressing becomes a real need. Needs confirmation before\n  either half (the `remark-gfm` wiring or the `table` handling) ships, since they have to land\n  together.",
    "title": "00C6JRYTMG00W",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:36:24.224Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G00Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G00Y",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Bold/italic: already fully correct today, independent of `remark-gfm`.** `**bold**`/\n  `*italic*` are core CommonMark — confirmed live (`strong`/`emphasis` mdast nodes) with plain\n  `remark-parse`, no extension needed. More importantly, it wouldn't matter either way: this\n  project never re-serializes inline content — `paragraph.text` (and every other leaf's `text`)\n  is a raw source-position slice, captured and replayed byte-for-byte regardless of whether\n  remark's own AST recognizes an inline span as `strong`/`emphasis`/anything else. The same\n  applies to strikethrough's actual *text* (the tildes survive verbatim in the raw slice whether\n  or not gfm is present) — gfm only changes whether remark's AST additionally recognizes it as a\n  distinct `delete` node internally, which nothing in this codebase reads today (inline-level\n  structural extraction — links, emphasis spans, etc. — remains the separately-tracked, unbuilt\n  \"Fractal Ontology Enhancements\" item). Confirmed live both with and without `remark-gfm` to be\n  sure this claim wasn't just assumed either.",
    "title": "00C6KAEKM800X",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:00:03.507Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G010",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**YAML frontmatter — parsing shape confirmed, schema representation is an open decision.**\n  See §5, its own section given the size of the decision.",
    "title": "00C6JCP37G010",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:08:03.410Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G011",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Confirmed parsing shape**: `remark-frontmatter` (now installed, tested, not yet wired into\n`astParser.ts`) with `.use(remarkFrontmatter, ['yaml'])` recognizes a leading `---\\n...\\n---`\nblock as its own mdast node, `{ type: 'yaml', value: '<raw yaml body, delimiters stripped>' }` —\nconfirmed live against a real multi-key example. Critically, this only *isolates* the block; it\ndoes **not** parse the YAML into an object — `.value` is still just a string (e.g.\n`\"title: My Doc\\ntags: [foo, bar]\\ndraft: true\"`). Turning that into real key/value data needs a\nYAML parser too (e.g. the `yaml` or `js-yaml` package — neither installed yet).",
    "title": "## 5. YAML frontmatter as `ArtifactNode` props — design, schema decision needed",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:08:03.410Z",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G012",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Confirmed parsing shape**: `remark-frontmatter` (now installed, tested, not yet wired into\n`astParser.ts`) with `.use(remarkFrontmatter, ['yaml'])` recognizes a leading `---\\n...\\n---`\nblock as its own mdast node, `{ type: 'yaml', value: '<raw yaml body, delimiters stripped>' }` —\nconfirmed live against a real multi-key example. Critically, this only *isolates* the block; it\ndoes **not** parse the YAML into an object — `.value` is still just a string (e.g.\n`\"title: My Doc\\ntags: [foo, bar]\\ndraft: true\"`). Turning that into real key/value data needs a\nYAML parser too (e.g. the `yaml` or `js-yaml` package — neither installed yet).",
    "title": "00C6JCP37G012",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:08:03.410Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G013",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G013",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Shape of the change, not yet built**:",
    "title": "00C6JCP37G013",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:08:03.410Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G014",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G014",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C6JCP37G014",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:08:03.410Z",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G015",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G015",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Frontmatter is file-level metadata, not a block — it should never become a `BlockNode` at all\n  (parallel to how `folders.ts` already treats a directory's own `README.md` specially, never\n  exposing it as a separate `ArtifactNode`). `parseMarkdownTree`'s signature would need to\n  change: return the parsed props alongside the root `BlockNode`, not fold them into the tree.\n  That ripples into every caller (`artifacts.ts`'s `ingestArtifact`, `verifyPhase0.ts`).",
    "title": "00C6JCP37G015",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:08:03.410Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G016",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G016",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Frontmatter is file-level metadata, not a block — it should never become a `BlockNode` at all\n  (parallel to how `folders.ts` already treats a directory's own `README.md` specially, never\n  exposing it as a separate `ArtifactNode`). `parseMarkdownTree`'s signature would need to\n  change: return the parsed props alongside the root `BlockNode`, not fold them into the tree.\n  That ripples into every caller (`artifacts.ts`'s `ingestArtifact`, `verifyPhase0.ts`).",
    "title": "00C6JCP37G016",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:08:03.410Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G017",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G017",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Open decision: how props are represented in `schema.json`.** Frontmatter keys/values are\n  inherently open-ended (whatever an author writes), which doesn't map cleanly onto typed\n  JSON-LD fields the way `ordered`/`start`/`checked` did (those are a fixed, known set). Two real\n  options, not yet chosen:",
    "title": "00C6JCP37G017",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:08:03.410Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G018",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G018",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Open decision: how props are represented in `schema.json`.** Frontmatter keys/values are\n  inherently open-ended (whatever an author writes), which doesn't map cleanly onto typed\n  JSON-LD fields the way `ordered`/`start`/`checked` did (those are a fixed, known set). Two real\n  options, not yet chosen:",
    "title": "00C6JCP37G018",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:08:03.410Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G019",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G019",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C6JCP37G019",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:08:03.410Z",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G01A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G01A",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**(A) One opaque field** — `ArtifactNode.props: Optional xsd:string`, holding the\n    JSON-serialized object. Simple, matches this project's existing precedent (every field on\n    `ArtifactNode` today is a plain scalar, nothing modeled as nested/typed graph data yet) —\n    but opaque to WOQL: no querying \"every artifact with `draft: true`\" without reading and\n    parsing every `props` string client-side.",
    "title": "00C6JCP37G01A",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:08:03.410Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G01B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G01B",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**(A) One opaque field** — `ArtifactNode.props: Optional xsd:string`, holding the\n    JSON-serialized object. Simple, matches this project's existing precedent (every field on\n    `ArtifactNode` today is a plain scalar, nothing modeled as nested/typed graph data yet) —\n    but opaque to WOQL: no querying \"every artifact with `draft: true`\" without reading and\n    parsing every `props` string client-side.",
    "title": "00C6JCP37G01B",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:08:03.410Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G01C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G01C",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**(B) Graph-native key/value pairs** — a `List`/`Set` of `{key, value}` subdocuments,\n    queryable directly via WOQL/GraphQL like any other field. More consistent with this\n    project's stated preference for graph-native structure over opaque blobs (`Assertion` was\n    built as a real edge specifically to avoid a flat-string anti-pattern, per\n    `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` Appendix B) — but real values aren't uniformly strings\n    (`tags` is a list, `draft` is a boolean), which a flat `{key: string, value: string}` pair\n    doesn't represent without picking a serialization/typing convention of its own, adding real\n    schema complexity for a feature with no current concrete query need driving it.",
    "title": "00C6JCP37G01C",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:08:03.410Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G01D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G01D",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**(B) Graph-native key/value pairs** — a `List`/`Set` of `{key, value}` subdocuments,\n    queryable directly via WOQL/GraphQL like any other field. More consistent with this\n    project's stated preference for graph-native structure over opaque blobs (`Assertion` was\n    built as a real edge specifically to avoid a flat-string anti-pattern, per\n    `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` Appendix B) — but real values aren't uniformly strings\n    (`tags` is a list, `draft` is a boolean), which a flat `{key: string, value: string}` pair\n    doesn't represent without picking a serialization/typing convention of its own, adding real\n    schema complexity for a feature with no current concrete query need driving it.",
    "title": "00C6JCP37G01D",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:08:03.410Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G01E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G01E",
    "children": [],
    "text": "No default is assumed here — this is exactly the kind of schema decision this project has\n    paused on before (the `Assertion`/`artifactId` key-strategy decisions earlier this session),\n    since getting it wrong costs a migration later, not just a rename.",
    "title": "00C6JCP37G01E",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:08:03.410Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G01F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G01F",
    "children": [],
    "text": "No default is assumed here — this is exactly the kind of schema decision this project has\n    paused on before (the `Assertion`/`artifactId` key-strategy decisions earlier this session),\n    since getting it wrong costs a migration later, not just a rename.",
    "title": "00C6JCP37G01F",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:08:03.410Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G01G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G01G",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Not implemented: needs the schema decision above, `remark-frontmatter` wired into the pipeline,\na YAML-parsing dependency added, and `parseMarkdownTree`'s signature (and callers) updated.",
    "title": "00C6JCP37G01G",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:08:03.410Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G01H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G01H",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Once §2's consuming rule ships, `project.ts`'s `serializeBlock` **must** change for `heading`/\n`serializeListItem` — today both build their output purely via `renderChildren`/recursion,\nnever reading `node.text` (confirmed by re-reading `project.ts` this session). Under the\n*copying* model that was safe (the paragraph was still there to recurse into); under the\n*consuming* model it would silently **drop the leading sentence from every projected document**,\nsince it no longer exists as a child at all. This is not an optional follow-up — it's required\nfor correctness the moment §2 is implemented, and should ship in the same change, not after.",
    "title": "## 6. Consequences for Artifact Projection (not yet made)",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:36:24.224Z",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G01J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G01J",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Once §2's consuming rule ships, `project.ts`'s `serializeBlock` **must** change for `heading`/\n`serializeListItem` — today both build their output purely via `renderChildren`/recursion,\nnever reading `node.text` (confirmed by re-reading `project.ts` this session). Under the\n*copying* model that was safe (the paragraph was still there to recurse into); under the\n*consuming* model it would silently **drop the leading sentence from every projected document**,\nsince it no longer exists as a child at all. This is not an optional follow-up — it's required\nfor correctness the moment §2 is implemented, and should ship in the same change, not after.",
    "title": "00C6JRYTMG01F",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:36:24.224Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G01K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G01K",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`reconcile.ts` matches `heading` by `.title` (Stage A) and `listItem` by type+position (Stage B)\n— neither key depends on the *value* of `.text`. So consuming the leading paragraph doesn't\nchange either node's own matching behavior. What it does change: today, that leading paragraph\nis a separate child, itself Stage-A-matched by *exact text equality* — editing just that\nsentence makes it fail to match (declined, shows as remove+add, per the reconciliation design's\n\"decline rather than guess\" principle) even though the heading/listItem around it is unchanged.\nOnce consumed, that same edit becomes an in-place field update on the already-matched\nheading/listItem (no separate node to fail-to-match) — arguably a more natural outcome (\"I\ntweaked the section's opening line\" reads as an edit, not a delete-and-recreate), but a real,\nworth-flagging shift in reconciliation stats for anything that edits leading sentences under\nheadings or list items.",
    "title": "## 7. Consequences for Reconciliation (a positive side effect, worth naming)",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:36:24.224Z",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G01M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6JCP37G01M",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`reconcile.ts` matches `heading` by `.title` (Stage A) and `listItem` by type+position (Stage B)\n— neither key depends on the *value* of `.text`. So consuming the leading paragraph doesn't\nchange either node's own matching behavior. What it does change: today, that leading paragraph\nis a separate child, itself Stage-A-matched by *exact text equality* — editing just that\nsentence makes it fail to match (declined, shows as remove+add, per the reconciliation design's\n\"decline rather than guess\" principle) even though the heading/listItem around it is unchanged.\nOnce consumed, that same edit becomes an in-place field update on the already-matched\nheading/listItem (no separate node to fail-to-match) — arguably a more natural outcome (\"I\ntweaked the section's opening line\" reads as an edit, not a delete-and-recreate), but a real,\nworth-flagging shift in reconciliation stats for anything that edits leading sentences under\nheadings or list items.",
    "title": "00C6JRYTMG01H",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:36:24.224Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6JCP37G01N",
