  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28001E",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**The same copy-not-consume gap §2 fixed for `heading`/`listItem` also exists here, unfixed.**\n  `readmeText = extractAbstract(parsedRoot)` (a read-only copy, same function, same issue) while\n  `readmeChildren = parsedRoot.children` keeps *every* parsed child unmodified — including the\n  same leading paragraph that was just copied into `text`. So today: full README content is\n  genuinely parsed and present (nothing is lost), but the leading paragraph is duplicated between\n  `FolderNode.text` and its first child, exactly the inconsistency §2 already fixed for\n  `heading`/`listItem` — this is the same fix, not yet applied to `folders.ts`.",
    "title": "00C6MMB19800B",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28001F",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**The same copy-not-consume gap §2 fixed for `heading`/`listItem` also exists here, unfixed.**\n  `readmeText = extractAbstract(parsedRoot)` (a read-only copy, same function, same issue) while\n  `readmeChildren = parsedRoot.children` keeps *every* parsed child unmodified — including the\n  same leading paragraph that was just copied into `text`. So today: full README content is\n  genuinely parsed and present (nothing is lost), but the leading paragraph is duplicated between\n  `FolderNode.text` and its first child, exactly the inconsistency §2 already fixed for\n  `heading`/`listItem` — this is the same fix, not yet applied to `folders.ts`.",
    "title": "00C6MMB19800C",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28001G",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`FolderNode` → README.md projection doesn't exist at all — a real, separate gap.**\n  `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A explicitly names this as part of Artifact Projection:\n  *\"For `FolderNode`s, the block children are serialized back out to the folder's `README.md`.\"*\n  Checked directly: `project.ts`/`kgCli.ts`/`graphql.ts` only ever handle `ArtifactNode` —\n  `projectArtifactToMarkdown`/`getArtifactTreeViaGraphQL` have no `FolderNode` path, and `kg:project`\n  has no way to target a folder. So \"full content parsed and round-tripped\" is half true: parsed,\n  yes; round-tripped, no — there is currently no way to regenerate a `README.md` from a\n  `FolderNode` at all. Not fixed here — flagged as its own gap, same size of effort as the\n  original Artifact Projection build, not a quick addition to this doc's scope.",
    "title": "00C6MMB19800D",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28001H",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`FolderNode` → README.md projection doesn't exist at all — a real, separate gap.**\n  `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A explicitly names this as part of Artifact Projection:\n  *\"For `FolderNode`s, the block children are serialized back out to the folder's `README.md`.\"*\n  Checked directly: `project.ts`/`kgCli.ts`/`graphql.ts` only ever handle `ArtifactNode` —\n  `projectArtifactToMarkdown`/`getArtifactTreeViaGraphQL` have no `FolderNode` path, and `kg:project`\n  has no way to target a folder. So \"full content parsed and round-tripped\" is half true: parsed,\n  yes; round-tripped, no — there is currently no way to regenerate a `README.md` from a\n  `FolderNode` at all. Not fixed here — flagged as its own gap, same size of effort as the\n  original Artifact Projection build, not a quick addition to this doc's scope.",
