      "BlockNode/00C6NCTH0G01P"
    ],
    "text": "Settled direction, designed now (not deferred): a generic, per-node `props` mechanism, so a new\npiece of type-specific metadata never again needs its own schema field. `ordered`/`start`/\n`checked` (shipped earlier this session) are exactly the pattern being moved away from — three\nseparate fields, one per feature — and **all three migrate onto `props`**, not just the two list\nadoption forces to move (see §8: an earlier revision of this doc left `checked` in place since\nnothing *forces* it to move, but there's no good reason to leave the one remaining hardcoded\nper-feature field once everything else is uniform — consistency, not necessity, is the actual\nargument, and it's the stronger one).",
    "title": "## 7. `props`: one extensible mechanism, not one field per feature",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28001K",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Settled direction, designed now (not deferred): a generic, per-node `props` mechanism, so a new\npiece of type-specific metadata never again needs its own schema field. `ordered`/`start`/\n`checked` (shipped earlier this session) are the pattern being moved away from — three separate\nfields, one per feature. **Not migrating them now** — they stay exactly as they are, unchanged,\nworking; this section designs the mechanism itself, for frontmatter (§5) and any future\nmetadata to use going forward.",
    "title": "00C6KGRJCR01M",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:27:11.251Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28001M",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Schema** (proposed):",
    "title": "00C6KX5Z4R01J",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:58:08.194Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28001N",
    "children": [],
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"Prop\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@subdocument\": [],\n  \"key\": \"xsd:string\",\n  \"value\": \"xsd:string\"\n}\n```",
    "title": "00C6KX5Z4R01K",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:58:08.194Z",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28001P",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001Q",
      "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001S",
      "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001V",
      "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG021",
      "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG023"
    ],
    "text": "- **`Prop` is abstract and carries only `key`** — deliberately, so `value`'s type is never fixed\n  at the mechanism's own level. This is the exact same pattern already used for `BaseEdge`\n  (abstract) → `Assertion` (the one concrete leaf, `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2.C-D): the\n  shared shape lives on the abstract parent, the concrete detail lives on whichever leaf\n  actually gets instantiated. Here it buys real freedom later — a future `BooleanProp` (`value:\n  xsd:boolean`) or `IntegerProp` (`value: xsd:integer`) could be added as another concrete leaf\n  of the same abstract `Prop`, genuinely WOQL-typed and queryable on `value` itself, without\n  touching `Prop` or anything already built on it — `BaseNode.props: Set<Prop>` is already\n  polymorphic over whatever concrete leaf a given prop actually is.\n- **For now, `StringProp` is the only concrete leaf, exactly as designed before the rename**:\n  `value` is a single `xsd:string`, JSON-encoded for anything that isn't naturally a string\n  (a boolean as `\"true\"`, a number as `\"3\"`, a list as `\"[\\\"foo\\\",\\\"bar\\\"]\"`), decoded at the\n  application layer. Every prop this doc actually needs today (`orderedList`, `startIndex`,\n  `checked`, frontmatter) uses `StringProp` — nothing forces reaching for a hypothetical typed\n  variant yet.\n- `@subdocument`, deliberately unlike `BaseLink`/`BaseEdge` — a prop is owned entirely by the one\n  node it belongs to, with no reification use case (nobody needs to assert something *about* a\n  prop the way `Assertion` lets you assert about an edge), so it doesn't need the global `@id`\n  `BaseLink`/`BaseEdge` specifically require for that (`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2's \"No\n  Subdocuments\" rule doesn't apply here — that rule's reasoning was reification, which doesn't\n  apply to `Prop`).\n- `key` stays a plain top-level field regardless of which concrete leaf carries a given prop, so\n  \"find every node with a `checked` prop\" is a real, direct WOQL triple match either way; only\n  `value` (and only for `StringProp`) stays opaque to the query engine.\n- Added to `BaseNode` (not just `BlockNode`) as `props: { \"@type\": \"Set\", \"@class\": \"Prop\" }`\n  (polymorphic over `Prop`'s concrete leaves; `Optional` in effect via an empty `Set`, no\n  migration needed for existing documents) — so any node kind (`BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode`,\n  `FolderNode`) can use the same mechanism, matching the user's own framing (\"ordered & start\n  for list, frontmatter for artifact\" — two different node kinds, one shared mechanism).",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G01H",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false,
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001P/props/StringProp/F4MU8ulrI75LGOZr",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001P/props/StringProp/WrI5GgTIpBNftR7H",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28001Q",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`Prop` is abstract and carries only `key`** — deliberately, so `value`'s type is never fixed\n  at the mechanism's own level. This is the exact same pattern already used for `BaseEdge`\n  (abstract) → `Assertion` (the one concrete leaf, `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2.C-D): the\n  shared shape lives on the abstract parent, the concrete detail lives on whichever leaf\n  actually gets instantiated. Here it buys real freedom later — a future `BooleanProp` (`value:\n  xsd:boolean`) or `IntegerProp` (`value: xsd:integer`) could be added as another concrete leaf\n  of the same abstract `Prop`, genuinely WOQL-typed and queryable on `value` itself, without\n  touching `Prop` or anything already built on it — `BaseNode.props: Set<Prop>` is already\n  polymorphic over whatever concrete leaf a given prop actually is.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G01J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28001R",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`@subdocument`, deliberately unlike `BaseLink`/`BaseEdge` — a prop is owned entirely by the one\n  node it belongs to, with no reification use case (nobody needs to assert something *about* a\n  prop the way `Assertion` lets you assert about an edge), so it doesn't need the global `@id`\n  `BaseLink`/`BaseEdge` specifically require for that (`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2's \"No\n  Subdocuments\" rule doesn't apply here — that rule's reasoning was reification, which doesn't\n  apply to `Prop`).",
    "title": "00C6MMB19800T",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28001S",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**For now, `StringProp` is the only concrete leaf, exactly as designed before the rename**:\n  `value` is a single `xsd:string`, JSON-encoded for anything that isn't naturally a string\n  (a boolean as `\"true\"`, a number as `\"3\"`, a list as `\"[\\\"foo\\\",\\\"bar\\\"]\"`), decoded at the\n  application layer. Every prop this doc actually needs today (`orderedList`, `startIndex`,\n  `checked`, frontmatter) uses `StringProp` — nothing forces reaching for a hypothetical typed\n  variant yet.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G01K",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28001T",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`value` is a single `xsd:string`, JSON-encoded — not a typed union — so it uniformly holds a\n  boolean (`\"true\"`), a number (`\"3\"`), a list (`\"[\\\"foo\\\",\\\"bar\\\"]\"`), or a raw opaque string\n  (frontmatter's case), decoded at the application layer rather than the schema needing to know\n  every value shape in advance. The tradeoff already named in §5 for a pure opaque blob (not\n  WOQL-queryable per-key) is *halved* here, not eliminated: `key` is a plain top-level field, so\n  \"find every node with a `checked` prop\" is a real, direct WOQL triple match; only the *value*\n  stays opaque to the query engine.",
    "title": "00C6KX5Z4R01R",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:58:08.194Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28001V",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`@subdocument`, deliberately unlike `BaseLink`/`BaseEdge` — a prop is owned entirely by the one\n  node it belongs to, with no reification use case (nobody needs to assert something *about* a\n  prop the way `Assertion` lets you assert about an edge), so it doesn't need the global `@id`\n  `BaseLink`/`BaseEdge` specifically require for that (`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2's \"No\n  Subdocuments\" rule doesn't apply here — that rule's reasoning was reification, which doesn't\n  apply to `Prop`).",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G01M",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28001W",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Added to `BaseNode` (not just `BlockNode`) as `props: { \"@type\": \"Set\", \"@class\": \"Prop\" }`\n  (`Optional` in effect via an empty `Set`, no migration needed for existing documents) — so any\n  node kind (`BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode`, `FolderNode`) can use the same mechanism, matching the\n  user's own framing (\"ordered & start for list, frontmatter for artifact\" — two different node\n  kinds, one shared mechanism).",
    "title": "00C6KX5Z4R01T",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:58:08.194Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28001X",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**\"Each type of block decides its own props\"** is enforced by convention in ingestion code\n(`astParser.ts` decides which keys a `list`/`listItem`/etc. populates), not by the schema —\nthe schema itself stays generic and open, consistent with this project's existing perimeter-\nboundary-transducer philosophy (type constraints belong to the code that populates a node, not\nto a rigid schema-level enum of allowed keys per type).",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G01R",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28001Y",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Not implemented: schema addition, and (separately, larger) migrating `ordered`/`start`/`checked`\nonto this mechanism is explicitly **not** part of this change — see above.",
    "title": "00C6K5Y28001Y",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:46:16.273Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28001Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28001Z",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C6MMB198016",
      "BlockNode/00C6MMB198017",
      "BlockNode/00C6MMB198018",
      "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R01N",
      "BlockNode/00C6KX5Z4R021",
      "BlockNode/00C6KX5Z4R022",
      "BlockNode/00C6KX5Z4R023",
      "BlockNode/00C6MH0GCG02F",
      "BlockNode/00C6KX5Z4R025"
    ],
    "text": "**The problem**: today, a `list` is always its own `BlockNode`, sitting between its logical\n\"owner\" (the paragraph/heading/listItem that introduces it) and its own `listItem` children —\nan artificial intermediate node the fractal-outliner model doesn't actually need in the common\ncase. Settled: a `list` should be **adopted** by whatever block immediately precedes it, when\nthat preceding sibling is a `paragraph`, `listItem`, or `heading` — its own `listItem`s become\ndirect children of that preceding sibling instead, and the `list` wrapper itself doesn't exist\nas a node at all. Only when no such preceding sibling exists (a list is the very first thing in\nits parent's children — e.g. the first content in a document, or the first thing inside a\nblockquote) does it remain an orphaned \"list block,\" exactly as today (no title, no text).",
    "title": "## 8. List refactor: adoption instead of a standalone list block",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y280020",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y280020",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**The problem**: today, a `list` is always its own `BlockNode`, sitting between its logical\n\"owner\" (the paragraph/heading/listItem that introduces it) and its own `listItem` children —\nan artificial intermediate node the fractal-outliner model doesn't actually need in the common\ncase. Settled: a `list` should be **adopted** by whatever block immediately precedes it, when\nthat preceding sibling is a `paragraph`, `listItem`, or `heading` — its own `listItem`s become\ndirect children of that preceding sibling instead, and the `list` wrapper itself doesn't exist\nas a node at all. Only when no such preceding sibling exists (a list is the very first thing in\nits parent's children — e.g. the first content in a document, or the first thing inside a\nblockquote) does it remain an orphaned \"list block,\" exactly as today (no title, no text).",
    "title": "00C6MMB198015",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y280021",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y280021",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Scope of \"immediately precedes,\" precisely**: the list's previous sibling *within its current\nparent's children, at the point `groupByHeadings` has already run* (so a heading's own nested\ncontent already sees its restructured, non-flat sibling list) — checked once per list, against\nthe original parse structure, not recomputed after other adoptions have already happened at the\nsame level. This matters for the common nested-list case: `- Parent item\\n  - Nested item`\ntoday produces `listItem(\"Parent item\") → [paragraph, list → [listItem(\"Nested item\")]]`; under\nadoption, since the nested list's immediately preceding sibling *is* `listItem`'s own leading\nparagraph (already consumed into `listItem.text` per §2), the nested list's own `listItem`s\nbecome direct children of the outer `listItem` — no intermediate `list` node for the ordinary\nsingle-nested-list-under-a-bullet case at all.",
    "title": "00C6MH0GCG02B",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:17:46.661Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y280022",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y280022",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Where do `ordered`/`start` live once the `list` node dissolves?** Not resolved here — this is\na real, direct consequence of adoption that needs an answer before implementing: either (a) the\nadopting parent gains `ordered`/`start` itself (describing \"how my adopted list items are\nnumbered\"), or (b) — more consistent with §7 — expressed as `props` entries on the adopting\nparent, which is exactly the kind of \"a block decides its own props\" case §7 exists for. Leaning\ntoward (b), since it avoids `ordered`/`start` meaning two different things depending on whether\na node is an un-adopted `list` (its own fields) or an adopting parent (borrowed fields) — but not\nsettled, flagged as the one open question this design leaves before it's implementable.",
    "title": "00C6K5Y280022",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:46:16.273Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y280023",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y280023",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Consequences flagged, not resolved**: Artifact Projection (`project.ts`) would need real\nrework — today, `serializeBlock`'s `list` case is the only place list-marker/indentation logic\nlives; under adoption, an ordinary `heading`/`paragraph`/`listItem` case would sometimes *also*\nneed to emit list-item markers for its adopted children, depending on where `ordered`/`start`\nend up living. Reconciliation (`reconcile.ts`) would also need to be re-examined — today a\n`list`'s own `listItem`s are matched (Stage B) as the `list`'s children; under adoption they\nbecome the adopting parent's children directly, changing which node's `diffChildren` call sees\nthem. Neither is designed here — this section settles the *adoption rule* itself; the\nprojection and reconciliation mechanics it requires are follow-on design work.",
    "title": "00C6K5Y280023",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:46:16.273Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y280024",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y280024",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Once §2's consuming rule ships, `project.ts`'s `serializeBlock` **must** change for `heading`/\n`serializeListItem` — today both build their output purely via `renderChildren`/recursion,\nnever reading `node.text` (confirmed by re-reading `project.ts` this session). Under the\n*copying* model that was safe (the paragraph was still there to recurse into); under the\n*consuming* model it would silently **drop the leading sentence from every projected document**,\nsince it no longer exists as a child at all. This is not an optional follow-up — it's required\nfor correctness the moment §2 is implemented, and should ship in the same change, not after.",
    "title": "## 9. Consequences for Artifact Projection (not yet made)",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y280025",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y280025",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Once §2's consuming rule ships, `project.ts`'s `serializeBlock` **must** change for `heading`/\n`serializeListItem` — today both build their output purely via `renderChildren`/recursion,\nnever reading `node.text` (confirmed by re-reading `project.ts` this session). Under the\n*copying* model that was safe (the paragraph was still there to recurse into); under the\n*consuming* model it would silently **drop the leading sentence from every projected document**,\nsince it no longer exists as a child at all. This is not an optional follow-up — it's required\nfor correctness the moment §2 is implemented, and should ship in the same change, not after.",
    "title": "00C6MMB19801M",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y280026",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y280026",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`reconcile.ts` matches `heading` by `.title` (Stage A) and `listItem` by type+position (Stage B)\n— neither key depends on the *value* of `.text`. So consuming the leading paragraph doesn't\nchange either node's own matching behavior. What it does change: today, that leading paragraph\nis a separate child, itself Stage-A-matched by *exact text equality* — editing just that\nsentence makes it fail to match (declined, shows as remove+add, per the reconciliation design's\n\"decline rather than guess\" principle) even though the heading/listItem around it is unchanged.\nOnce consumed, that same edit becomes an in-place field update on the already-matched\nheading/listItem (no separate node to fail-to-match) — arguably a more natural outcome (\"I\ntweaked the section's opening line\" reads as an edit, not a delete-and-recreate), but a real,\nworth-flagging shift in reconciliation stats for anything that edits leading sentences under\nheadings or list items.",
    "title": "## 10. Consequences for Reconciliation (a positive side effect, worth naming)",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y280027",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y280027",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`reconcile.ts` matches `heading` by `.title` (Stage A) and `listItem` by type+position (Stage B)\n— neither key depends on the *value* of `.text`. So consuming the leading paragraph doesn't\nchange either node's own matching behavior. What it does change: today, that leading paragraph\nis a separate child, itself Stage-A-matched by *exact text equality* — editing just that\nsentence makes it fail to match (declined, shows as remove+add, per the reconciliation design's\n\"decline rather than guess\" principle) even though the heading/listItem around it is unchanged.\nOnce consumed, that same edit becomes an in-place field update on the already-matched\nheading/listItem (no separate node to fail-to-match) — arguably a more natural outcome (\"I\ntweaked the section's opening line\" reads as an edit, not a delete-and-recreate), but a real,\nworth-flagging shift in reconciliation stats for anything that edits leading sentences under\nheadings or list items.",
    "title": "00C6MMB19801P",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y280028",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y280028",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R020",
      "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R021",
      "BlockNode/00C6Q3P898011",
      "BlockNode/00C6Q3P898012",
      "BlockNode/00C6Q3P898013"
    ],
    "text": "**Implemented and verified**: §1 (uniform title/text semantics); §2 (heading/listItem consume,\nshallow — `convertChildren` in `astParser.ts`); §3 (blockquote, opaque leaf); §4's\n`thematicBreak`/`html`/`table` + `remark-gfm` wiring; §5 (frontmatter, extracted via\n`remark-frontmatter` and returned separately from `parseMarkdownTree` as `{root, frontmatter}`,\nrealized as one `props` entry on both `ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode`); §6's first finding\n(the same copy-not-consume fix applied to `folders.ts`'s README absorption, including transferring\nany list the consumed leading paragraph had itself adopted up to the `FolderNode` itself); §7\n(the full `Prop`/`StringProp` schema, `BaseNode.props`, the shared `getProp`/`setProp` helper in\n`props.ts`); §8 (list adoption — including the heading/listItem consume-then-adopt symmetry, the\n`orderedList`/`startIndex` rename, and the two-adjacent-lists orphaning case found during\nimplementation); §9 (`project.ts`'s `serializeBlock`/`renderChildren`/`serializeListItem`\ngeneralized for consumed text and adopted-listItem runs, reading `orderedList`/`startIndex`/\n`checked` from `props`). `verify:phase0 -- --db` clean end-to-end (AST transduction, projection\nround-trip, live TerminusDB read/write via the new GraphQL `props`/`_json` path, reconciliation,\nFolderNode ingestion, Assertion CRUD); `npm run build` clean.",
    "title": "## 11. Status",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y280029",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y280029",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Implemented and verified**: `thematicBreak`/`html` (§4) and `table` + `remark-gfm` wiring\n(§4, also genuinely fixes `checked`) — `astParser.ts`, `reconcile.ts`, `project.ts` all updated,\n`verify:phase0 -- --db` clean, sample markdown exercises all of it, `checked`'s actual value now\nasserted (not just round-trip shape).",
    "title": "00C6KX5Z4R02B",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:58:08.194Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28002A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28002A",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Decided, not yet implemented**: §1 (uniform title/text semantics); §2 (heading/listItem\nconsume, shallow — supersedes the copying version shipped earlier this session); §6 (same\ncopy-not-consume fix, applied to `folders.ts`'s README absorption); §7 (`Prop`\nsubdocument/`props` mechanism, schema designed, not migrating `ordered`/`start`/`checked` yet);\n§5 (frontmatter realized as one `props` entry, not a bespoke field, once §7 ships).",
    "title": "00C6K5Y28002A",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:46:16.273Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28002B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28002B",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Designed, one open question remaining before it's implementable**: §8 (list adoption) — the\nrule itself is settled, but where `ordered`/`start` live once a `list` node can dissolve is not\nyet decided (leaning toward §7's `props`, not settled), and its Artifact Projection/reconciliation\nconsequences are named but not yet designed.",
    "title": "00C6K5Y28002B",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T18:46:16.273Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28002C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28002C",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Investigated, real gap, not yet designed**: §6's second finding — `FolderNode` → `README.md`\nprojection doesn't exist at all (only `ArtifactNode` projection does), despite being named in\n`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A. Separate effort from the copy-not-consume fix above.",
    "title": "00C6MMB19801V",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28002D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28002D",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Deferred deliberately**: §3 (blockquote); inline features (including wikilinks, §4) confirmed\npermanently out of scope — no future revisit implied.",
    "title": "00C6MBBAGG02Y",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:10:30.322Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6K5Y28002E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6K5Y28002E",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Required once §2 ships**: §6 (`project.ts` changes for `heading`/`listItem`).",
    "title": "00C6MBBAGG02Z",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:10:30.322Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KAEKM801Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KAEKM801Y",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`ordered`/`start` are this mechanism's first real user — decided in §8, not deferred.** An\nearlier revision of this doc treated migrating `ordered`/`start`/`checked` onto `props` as a\nsingle, indefinitely-deferred future item. Corrected: `ordered`/`start` migrate now, as part of\n§8's list-adoption design — once a `list` node can dissolve, its `ordered`/`start` need\nsomewhere to live that isn't a `list`-only field, and `props` is exactly that. `checked` is\ndifferent and genuinely stays put — see §8's reasoning (it lives on `listItem`, which adoption\nnever dissolves, only relocates).",
    "title": "00C6KGRJCR01Z",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:27:11.251Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KAEKM801Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KAEKM801Z",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Not implemented: schema addition (`Prop` class, `BaseNode.props`), and the `ordered`/`start`\nmigration itself, which is coupled to §8's still-not-fully-designed projection/reconciliation\nconsequences.",
    "title": "00C6KGRJCR020",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:27:11.251Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KAEKM8023",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KAEKM8023",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Where `ordered`/`start` live once the `list` node dissolves — decided.** `ordered`/`start`\nbecome `props` (§7) of whichever block *now functions as a list* — the adopting parent when\nadoption happens, or the orphaned `list` block itself when it doesn't (a bare, un-adopted `list`\ngets exactly the same treatment as an adopting parent, for the same reason: it's the node that\n\"is now a list\"). This means `ordered`/`start` **do** migrate off their current hardcoded\n`BlockNode` fields onto `props` uniformly — not left in place for the un-adopted case as an\nearlier revision of this doc assumed by default. This is the first real user of §7's mechanism,\nmotivated directly by adoption needing somewhere for these two fields to go once the `list` node\nthat used to hold them can disappear.",
    "title": "00C6KGRJCR024",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:27:11.251Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KAEKM8024",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KAEKM8024",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`checked` is unaffected — no migration.** Unlike `ordered`/`start` (which lived on the `list`\nwrapper, the very node adoption dissolves), `checked` lives on `listItem` itself — and `listItem`\nnodes are never dissolved by adoption, only relocated (their parent changes from the dissolving\n`list` to whatever adopted it). There's no node disappearing out from under `checked`, so it\nstays exactly where it is, a plain `BlockNode` field, untouched.",
    "title": "00C6KGRJCR025",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:27:11.251Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KAEKM8025",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KAEKM8025",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Consequences flagged, not resolved**: Artifact Projection (`project.ts`) needs real rework —\ntoday, `serializeBlock`'s `list` case is the only place list-marker/indentation logic lives;\nunder adoption, an ordinary `heading`/`paragraph`/`listItem` case would sometimes *also* need to\nemit list-item markers for its adopted children, reading `ordered`/`start` from its own `props`\nrather than from a `list` node's fields. Reconciliation (`reconcile.ts`) also needs\nre-examination — today a `list`'s own `listItem`s are matched (Stage B) as the `list`'s children;\nunder adoption they become the adopting parent's children directly, changing which node's\n`diffChildren` call sees them. Neither is designed here — this section settles the *adoption\nrule* and where `ordered`/`start` go; the projection and reconciliation mechanics themselves are\nfollow-on design work.",
    "title": "00C6KGRJCR026",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:27:11.251Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KAEKM802C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KAEKM802C",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Decided, not yet implemented**: §1 (uniform title/text semantics); §2 (heading/listItem\nconsume, shallow — supersedes the copying version shipped earlier this session); §6 (same\ncopy-not-consume fix, applied to `folders.ts`'s README absorption); §7 (`Prop`\nsubdocument/`props` mechanism — `ordered`/`start` are its first real migration target, per §8,\nnot indefinitely deferred; `checked` stays a plain field, unaffected); §5 (frontmatter realized\nas one `props` entry, not a bespoke field, once §7 ships); §8 (list adoption rule, and where\n`ordered`/`start` go — both now decided).",
    "title": "00C6KGRJCR02D",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:27:11.251Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KAEKM802D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KAEKM802D",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Designed, real consequences still open**: §8's projection/reconciliation mechanics\n(`project.ts` needs to emit list markers from non-`list` nodes' `props`; `reconcile.ts` needs\nre-examination for who owns a `listItem` after adoption) are named, not yet designed themselves.",
    "title": "00C6KGRJCR02E",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:27:11.251Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KAEKM802E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KAEKM802E",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Also confirmed documented but unimplemented (pre-existing, not new)**: inline link extraction\ninto `BaseLink`/`BlockNode.links` — `Aperas-fractal-ontology-implementation-plan.md` Stage 2 item\n4, already tracked in `Aperas-dev-status.md`'s \"Fractal Ontology Enhancements\" — covers both\nordinary Markdown links and (once a wikilink plugin is added) wikilinks alike; not a new gap,\njust confirmed still real and still unimplemented.",
