  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KGRJCR00V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KGRJCR00V",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Bold/italic/strikethrough/footnotes: none of these are block-level, none go in `isStructural`,\nnone get special-cased — confirmed correct today regardless (`**bold**`/`*italic*` are core\nCommonMark; strikethrough's literal text survives verbatim in the raw slice with or without\n`remark-gfm` either way, since this project never re-serializes inline content — every leaf's\n`text` is a raw source-slice, replayed byte-for-byte, independent of what remark's own AST\nrecognizes inline). This is a closed decision, not a gap — inline *structural* extraction of\naddressable spans is a different, separately-tracked concern (Fractal Ontology Enhancements).",
    "title": "### Decided — inline content is out of scope, permanently",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KGRJCR00W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KGRJCR00W",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Bold/italic/strikethrough/footnotes: none of these are block-level, none go in `isStructural`,\nnone get special-cased — confirmed correct today regardless (`**bold**`/`*italic*` are core\nCommonMark; strikethrough's literal text survives verbatim in the raw slice with or without\n`remark-gfm` either way, since this project never re-serializes inline content — every leaf's\n`text` is a raw source-slice, replayed byte-for-byte, independent of what remark's own AST\nrecognizes inline). This is a closed decision, not a gap — inline *structural* extraction of\naddressable spans is a different, separately-tracked concern (Fractal Ontology Enhancements).",
    "title": "00C6MMB19000X",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KGRJCR00X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KGRJCR00X",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Links are **not** in the \"permanently out of scope\" bucket above — unlike bold/italic/etc.,\nextracting them is an already-designed, still-unbuilt feature\n(`Aperas-fractal-ontology-implementation-plan.md` Stage 2 item 4; tracked in\n`Aperas-dev-status.md`'s \"Fractal Ontology Enhancements\"): inline links become `BaseLink` objects\nin `BlockNode.links`, `BaseLink.target: BaseNode` pointing at a real node.",
    "title": "### Designed, not yet implemented — `BlockNode.links` extraction scope",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KGRJCR00Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KGRJCR00Y",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Links are **not** in the \"permanently out of scope\" bucket above — unlike bold/italic/etc.,\nextracting them is an already-designed, still-unbuilt feature\n(`Aperas-fractal-ontology-implementation-plan.md` Stage 2 item 4; tracked in\n`Aperas-dev-status.md`'s \"Fractal Ontology Enhancements\"): inline links become `BaseLink` objects\nin `BlockNode.links`, `BaseLink.target: BaseNode` pointing at a real node.",
    "title": "00C6MMB19000Z",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KGRJCR00Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KGRJCR00Z",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Scope, clarified this turn**: extraction isn't wikilink-specific — it applies to *any* link,\nregardless of syntax, whose target resolves to an internal node reference (a snowflake code),\nnot just `[[wikilink]]` syntax. An ordinary Markdown link, `[text](url)`, is exactly as eligible\nas a wikilink when its target is (or resolves to) an internal code — the deciding factor is what\nthe target *is* (internal node vs. external URL), not which bracket syntax produced it. A link\nto an external URL never becomes a `BaseLink` regardless of syntax; there's nothing internal to\ntarget.",
    "title": "00C6KGRJCR00Z",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:27:11.251Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KGRJCR010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KGRJCR010",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Confirmed empirically, not yet built: ordinary `[text](url)` links already parse as a proper\n`link` mdast node under plain `remark-parse` (core CommonMark) — the parsing half is free.\nWikilink syntax (`[[Page]]`, `[[Page|Alias]]`) parses as nothing at all today — no plugin\ninstalled, no CommonMark/GFM rule covers it, so it's literal text, brackets included. Needed to\nactually build this: a wikilink plugin (e.g. `remark-wiki-link`) so `[[...]]` reaches the same\nrecognized-node starting point `[text](url)` already has, then one shared piece of extraction\nlogic — per recognized link (either syntax), decide if its target is an internal code and if so\nemit a `BaseLink` — rather than two separate, syntax-specific extraction paths.",
    "title": "00C6KGRJCR010",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:27:11.251Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KGRJCR011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KGRJCR011",
    "children": [],
    "text": "YAML frontmatter — parsing shape confirmed, stage-appropriate design settled. See §5.",
    "title": "### Pointer",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KGRJCR012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KGRJCR012",
    "children": [],
    "text": "YAML frontmatter — parsing shape confirmed, stage-appropriate design settled. See §5.",
    "title": "00C6MMB190015",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KGRJCR02F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KGRJCR02F",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Pre-existing, confirmed not new**: `BlockNode.links` extraction (§4's last subsection) —\nalready tracked in `Aperas-dev-status.md`, scope clarified this turn (any link with an internal\ntarget, not wikilink-specific), still unimplemented.",
    "title": "00C6MMB19801T",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KX5Z4R00V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KX5Z4R00V",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Scope, clarified this turn**: extraction isn't wikilink-specific — it applies to *any* link\nwhose target resolves to an internal node reference (a snowflake code), not just wikilink syntax\nused on its own. The deciding factor is what the target *is* (internal code vs. external URL).\nA link to an external URL never becomes a `BaseLink`; there's nothing internal to target.",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG00K",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KX5Z4R00W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KX5Z4R00W",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Corrected, confirmed live**: `[[...]]` isn't a separate, competing link syntax needing its own\nplugin — it's how the *target* of an ordinary link is written to signal \"this is an internal\ncode, not a URL\": `[internal link]([[00C5H15NT0000]])`. Tested directly: plain `remark-parse`\n(no plugin, no `remark-gfm`) already parses this as a completely ordinary `link` mdast node —\n`{ url: \"[[00C5H15NT0000]]\", children: [{ type: \"text\", value: \"internal link\" }] }` — exactly\nthe same node shape as `[external](https://example.com)` (confirmed side-by-side in the same\ntest), just with a different-looking `url` string. So the parsing half is *entirely* free\nalready, for both forms — no wikilink plugin needed at all for this project's actual usage. An\nearlier revision of this doc wrongly assumed `[[...]]` would be used the Obsidian/Logseq way (as\na standalone replacement for `[text](url)`, tested in isolation as bare `[[Some Page]]`) and\nconcluded a plugin like `remark-wiki-link` was required — that conclusion doesn't apply to the\nform actually being designed here.",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG00M",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KX5Z4R00X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KX5Z4R00X",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Still to build: one extraction pass over every `link` node (all of them already parse\ncorrectly) that pattern-matches its `url` against `^\\[\\[(.+)\\]\\]$` — a match means \"internal\ncode, strip the brackets, treat the inner text as a target reference and emit a `BaseLink`\";\nno match means \"external URL, leave as plain inline text, same as today.\" One shared check,\nnot two syntax-specific paths — the bracket-wrapped-target convention *is* the recognition\nmechanism, nothing else needed to distinguish internal from external.",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG00N",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KX5Z4R01H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KX5Z4R01H",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Settled direction, designed now (not deferred): a generic, per-node `props` mechanism, so a new\npiece of type-specific metadata never again needs its own schema field. `ordered`/`start`/\n`checked` (shipped earlier this session) are exactly the pattern being moved away from — three\nseparate fields, one per feature — and **all three migrate onto `props`**, not just the two list\nadoption forces to move (see §8: an earlier revision of this doc left `checked` in place since\nnothing *forces* it to move, but there's no good reason to leave the one remaining hardcoded\nper-feature field once everything else is uniform — consistency, not necessity, is the actual\nargument, and it's the stronger one).",
    "title": "00C6MMB19800G",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KX5Z4R01W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KX5Z4R01W",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`orderedList`/`startIndex` are this mechanism's first real user — decided in §8, not\ndeferred.** (Renamed from `ordered`/`start` — see §8.) An earlier revision of this doc treated\nmigrating these onto `props` as a single, indefinitely-deferred future item. Corrected: they\nmigrate now, as part of §8's list-adoption design — once a `list` node can dissolve, they need\nsomewhere to live that isn't a `list`-only field, and `props` is exactly that. `checked` joins\nthem (§8) for consistency, not because anything forces it to.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G01S",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KX5Z4R01X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KX5Z4R01X",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Not implemented: schema addition (`Prop` class, `BaseNode.props`), and the migration itself,\nwhich is coupled to §8's projection/reconciliation mechanics (now designed, see §8/§9).",
    "title": "00C6MMB198012",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KX5Z4R021",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KX5Z4R021",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Renamed for clarity: `ordered`/`start` → `orderedList`/`startIndex`.** Once these can live on\na node that isn't itself a `list` (an adopting `heading`/`paragraph`/`listItem`), the old names\nread wrong — \"`ordered`\" on a heading's `props` doesn't say what it's describing. `orderedList`/\n`startIndex` read correctly regardless of which node kind carries them.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G027",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KX5Z4R022",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KX5Z4R022",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Where they live once the `list` node dissolves — decided.** `orderedList`/`startIndex` become\n`props` (§7) of whichever block *now functions as a list* — the adopting parent when adoption\nhappens, or the orphaned `list` block itself when it doesn't (uniform treatment, not a special\ncase for either side). This is `props`'s first real user, motivated directly by adoption needing\nsomewhere for these two fields to go once the `list` node that used to hold them can disappear.\nA node can only ever end up hosting *one* logical list's worth of adopted `listItem`s, never two\nunrelated ones needing different `orderedList`/`startIndex` values — because adoption is checked\nper-list against that specific list's own unique immediately-preceding sibling; two sibling\nlists can never share the same immediate predecessor (something — even just the first list\nitself — always separates them), so one set of `props` per node is always enough.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G028",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KX5Z4R023",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KX5Z4R023",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`checked` migrates too, for consistency, not necessity.** An earlier revision of this doc left\n`checked` on `listItem` as a plain field, reasoning that adoption never dissolves `listItem`\nnodes so nothing *forces* it to move. True, but incomplete: once `orderedList`/`startIndex` are\non `props`, leaving `checked` as the one remaining hardcoded per-feature field is inconsistent\nwith the whole point of §7 — one mechanism, not \"some metadata uses it, some doesn't.\" `checked`\nmoves too.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G029",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KX5Z4R024",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KX5Z4R024",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Artifact Projection — designed, not just flagged.** Today, `serializeBlock`'s `list` case is\nthe only place list-marker/indentation logic lives, reading `node.ordered`/`node.start` directly\nand iterating `node.children` (all `listItem`s, homogeneous). Under adoption, that's no longer\nenough — a `heading`/`paragraph`/`listItem`'s own `children` can be a *mix* of ordinary blocks\nand a contiguous run of adopted `listItem`s. Proposed: generalize `renderChildren` (used by every\ncase, not just `list`) to scan its node's `children` for contiguous runs of `type === 'listItem'`\n— render each such run exactly like today's `list` case does (via `serializeListItem`, reading\n`orderedList`/`startIndex` from *this* node's own `props`, `checked` from each item's own\n`props`), and render everything else via the existing per-type dispatch, one block at a time.\nThis subsumes the old `list` case entirely rather than sitting alongside it: an orphaned `list`\nis just the special case where the *entire* `children` array is one such run — no separate case\nneeded. A run's position within `children` isn't fixed (adopted items could be first, last, or\nin the middle, depending on what the original list's preceding sibling actually was), so the\nscan has to check every position, not just assume the end.",
    "title": "00C6KX5Z4R024",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:58:08.194Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KX5Z4R025",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KX5Z4R025",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Reconciliation — designed, and it turns out nothing needs to change.** `diffChildren` already\noperates generically on whatever `children` array it's handed — it doesn't know or care whether\na `listItem` used to live under a `list` wrapper. `listItem` is already in `CONTAINER_TYPES`\n(Stage B: matched by type and relative position within the segments Stage A's leaf-anchors\ndefine), and that classification is keyed purely on `.type`, unaffected by *whose* children array\na node sits in. So adopted `listItem`s mixed in among an adopting parent's other children match\nexactly the same way they already do today as a `list`'s own children — grouped by type within\nwhichever segment they fall into. No changes needed to `reconcile.ts` at all; this was flagged\nas an open risk in an earlier revision of this doc before actually re-reading `diffChildren`\nclosely enough to confirm it.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G02D",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6KX5Z4R02C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6KX5Z4R02C",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Decided, not yet implemented**: §1 (uniform title/text semantics); §2 (heading/listItem\nconsume, shallow — supersedes the copying version shipped earlier this session); §4's\n`BlockNode.links` scope (any link with an internal-code target, either bracket form — no\nwikilink plugin needed after all, see §4); §6 (same copy-not-consume fix, applied to\n`folders.ts`'s README absorption); §7 (`Prop` subdocument/`props` mechanism — `orderedList`/\n`startIndex`/`checked` *all* migrate onto it, none left behind); §5 (frontmatter realized as one\n`props` entry, not a bespoke field, once §7 ships); §8 (list adoption rule, the\n`orderedList`/`startIndex` rename, and both projection and reconciliation mechanics — the latter\nturning out to need no code changes at all).",
    "title": "00C6KX5Z4R02C",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T19:58:08.194Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG00G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG00G",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Settled: `blockquote` is a true opaque leaf, the same category as `code`/`table`/\n`thematicBreak`/`html` — `text = rawText` (the full raw slice, markers included, as the source\nwrote them) and **no children at all**, regardless of what's actually nested inside it. A\nblockquote *can* contain a full sub-tree in theory (nested lists, multiple paragraphs, further\nstructure) — but doing so is a discouraged counter-pattern, not something this ontology\ndecomposes. Authors should be warned against putting structured content inside a blockquote;\nthe system doesn't attempt to structurally model it either way.",
    "title": "## 3. `blockquote`: decided — opaque leaf, structured content inside is a counter-pattern",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:10:30.322Z",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG00H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG00H",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Settled: `blockquote` is a true opaque leaf, the same category as `code`/`table`/\n`thematicBreak`/`html` — `text = rawText` (the full raw slice, markers included, as the source\nwrote them) and **no children at all**, regardless of what's actually nested inside it. A\nblockquote *can* contain a full sub-tree in theory (nested lists, multiple paragraphs, further\nstructure) — but doing so is a discouraged counter-pattern, not something this ontology\ndecomposes. Authors should be warned against putting structured content inside a blockquote;\nthe system doesn't attempt to structurally model it either way.",
    "title": "00C6MBBAGG00H",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:10:30.322Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG00J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG00J",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Implemented and verified**: `astParser.ts`'s `convertAstNode` no longer recurses into a\n`blockquote`'s children at all (forced to `[]`, the same treatment `table`'s `tableRow`/\n`tableCell` already get by simply not being structural — here made explicit, since blockquote's\ninner content *would* otherwise recurse, using the same node types as everything else).\n`reconcile.ts`'s `blockquote` moved from `CONTAINER_TYPES` to `LEAF_TYPES` (it now has real\n`text` and no children, exactly like any other leaf — matched by content equality, not\ntype+position). `project.ts`'s `blockquote` case now reads `node.text` directly (strip-then-\nreapply `> ` markers, same normalization as before) instead of recursing into now-empty\nchildren. Verified: `verify:phase0 -- --db` clean (one fewer block per blockquote in the sample,\nas expected — its former paragraph child no longer exists as a separate node).",
    "title": "00C6MBBAGG00J",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:10:30.322Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG012",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Authoring convention, settled — bare `[[code]]` never needs parser support at all.** The\ncanonical stored form is always `[title]([[code]])` — a human never has to hand-type that. The\n(unbuilt) Human UI is where a bare `[[code]]` — typed or pasted — gets auto-expanded into the\nfull `[title]([[code]])` form, filling in `title` for the human, before it's ever saved as\nMarkdown. This closes the wikilink question definitively: there is no scenario where the\nMarkdown *parser* needs to understand standalone `[[...]]` on its own — that form only exists\ntransiently as a UI input convenience, never in a saved file. A wikilink plugin genuinely isn't\nneeded, not now and not later, unless a future decision changes what gets *saved*.",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG00P",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG01Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG01Q",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Schema** (revised — abstract base + one concrete leaf, mirroring `BaseEdge`/`Assertion`):",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G01E",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG01R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG01R",
    "children": [],
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"Prop\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@abstract\": [],\n  \"@subdocument\": [],\n  \"key\": \"xsd:string\"\n}\n```",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G01F",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG01S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG01S",
    "children": [],
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"StringProp\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@subdocument\": [],\n  \"@inherits\": [\"Prop\"],\n  \"value\": \"xsd:string\"\n}\n```",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G01G",
    "type": "code",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG01W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG01W",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`Prop` is abstract and carries only `key`** — deliberately, so `value`'s type is never fixed\n  at the mechanism's own level. This is the exact same pattern already used for `BaseEdge`\n  (abstract) → `Assertion` (the one concrete leaf, `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2.C-D): the\n  shared shape lives on the abstract parent, the concrete detail lives on whichever leaf\n  actually gets instantiated. Here it buys real freedom later — a future `BooleanProp` (`value:\n  xsd:boolean`) or `IntegerProp` (`value: xsd:integer`) could be added as another concrete leaf\n  of the same abstract `Prop`, genuinely WOQL-typed and queryable on `value` itself, without\n  touching `Prop` or anything already built on it — `BaseNode.props: Set<Prop>` is already\n  polymorphic over whatever concrete leaf a given prop actually is.",
    "title": "00C6MMB19800P",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG01Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG01Y",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**For now, `StringProp` is the only concrete leaf, exactly as designed before the rename**:\n  `value` is a single `xsd:string`, JSON-encoded for anything that isn't naturally a string\n  (a boolean as `\"true\"`, a number as `\"3\"`, a list as `\"[\\\"foo\\\",\\\"bar\\\"]\"`), decoded at the\n  application layer. Every prop this doc actually needs today (`orderedList`, `startIndex`,\n  `checked`, frontmatter) uses `StringProp` — nothing forces reaching for a hypothetical typed\n  variant yet.",
    "title": "00C6MMB19800R",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG021",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG021",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`key` stays a plain top-level field regardless of which concrete leaf carries a given prop, so\n  \"find every node with a `checked` prop\" is a real, direct WOQL triple match either way; only\n  `value` (and only for `StringProp`) stays opaque to the query engine.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G01N",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG022",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG022",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`key` stays a plain top-level field regardless of which concrete leaf carries a given prop, so\n  \"find every node with a `checked` prop\" is a real, direct WOQL triple match either way; only\n  `value` (and only for `StringProp`) stays opaque to the query engine.",
    "title": "00C6MMB19800W",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG023",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG023",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Added to `BaseNode` (not just `BlockNode`) as `props: { \"@type\": \"Set\", \"@class\": \"Prop\" }`\n  (polymorphic over `Prop`'s concrete leaves; `Optional` in effect via an empty `Set`, no\n  migration needed for existing documents) — so any node kind (`BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode`,\n  `FolderNode`) can use the same mechanism, matching the user's own framing (\"ordered & start\n  for list, frontmatter for artifact\" — two different node kinds, one shared mechanism).",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G01P",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG024",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG024",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Added to `BaseNode` (not just `BlockNode`) as `props: { \"@type\": \"Set\", \"@class\": \"Prop\" }`\n  (polymorphic over `Prop`'s concrete leaves; `Optional` in effect via an empty `Set`, no\n  migration needed for existing documents) — so any node kind (`BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode`,\n  `FolderNode`) can use the same mechanism, matching the user's own framing (\"ordered & start\n  for list, frontmatter for artifact\" — two different node kinds, one shared mechanism).",
    "title": "00C6MMB19800Y",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG02E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG02E",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Artifact Projection — designed, not just flagged.** Today, `serializeBlock`'s `list` case is\nthe only place list-marker/indentation logic lives, reading `node.ordered`/`node.start` directly\nand iterating `node.children` (all `listItem`s, homogeneous). Under adoption, that's no longer\nenough — a `heading`/`paragraph`/`listItem`'s own `children` can be a *mix* of ordinary blocks\nand a contiguous run of adopted `listItem`s. Proposed: generalize `renderChildren` (used by every\ncase, not just `list`) to scan its node's `children` for contiguous runs of `type === 'listItem'`\n— render each such run exactly like today's `list` case does (via `serializeListItem`, reading\n`orderedList`/`startIndex` from *this* node's own `props`, `checked` from each item's own\n`props`), and render everything else via the existing per-type dispatch, one block at a time.\nThis subsumes the old `list` case entirely rather than sitting alongside it: an orphaned `list`\nis just the special case where the *entire* `children` array is one such run — no separate case\nneeded. The scan checks every position rather than assuming the run is always last, but in\npractice a *mid-children* run is a counter-pattern, not a case worth designing around as if it\nwere normal:",
    "title": "00C6MBBAGG02E",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:10:30.322Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG02F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG02F",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C6MMB19801D",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG02G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG02G",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`paragraph`**: structurally can't happen at all. A paragraph never has children of its own\n  except an adopted list — nothing else ever becomes a paragraph's child — so its `children` is\n  either empty or *entirely* one run. There's no \"after\" to worry about.",
    "title": "00C6MMB19801E",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG02H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG02H",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`paragraph`**: structurally can't happen at all. A paragraph never has children of its own\n  except an adopted list — nothing else ever becomes a paragraph's child — so its `children` is\n  either empty or *entirely* one run. There's no \"after\" to worry about.",
    "title": "00C6MMB19801F",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG02J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG02J",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`heading`/`listItem`**: *can* have other genuine children (sub-headings, more prose) after\n  an adopted list, since these node kinds do have real structural children beyond what any list\n  contributes — but doing so is a discouraged anti-pattern: once a section's content becomes\n  a list, further prose at the same level reads better as a new section (a following heading)\n  than as more content trailing after a list under the same one. Worth a warning to authors, not\n  a reason to complicate the algorithm — the general \"scan every position\" design already\n  handles it correctly if it happens anyway, which is the right way round: correct by\n  construction for the exceptional case, without needing to special-case *for* it.",
    "title": "00C6MMB19801G",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MBBAGG02K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MBBAGG02K",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`heading`/`listItem`**: *can* have other genuine children (sub-headings, more prose) after\n  an adopted list, since these node kinds do have real structural children beyond what any list\n  contributes — but doing so is a discouraged counter-pattern: once a section's content becomes\n  a list, further prose at the same level reads better as a new section (a following heading)\n  than as more content trailing after a list under the same one. Worth a warning to authors, not\n  a reason to complicate the algorithm — the general \"scan every position\" design already\n  handles it correctly if it happens anyway, which is the right way round: correct by\n  construction for the exceptional case, without needing to special-case *for* it.",
