  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MH0GCG02W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MH0GCG02W",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Decided, not yet implemented**: §1 (uniform title/text semantics); §2 (heading/listItem\nconsume, shallow — supersedes the copying version shipped earlier this session); §4's\n`BlockNode.links` scope (any link with an internal-code target, either bracket form — no\nwikilink plugin needed *ever*, since the bare form only exists as a Human UI input convenience,\nnever in saved Markdown); §6 (same copy-not-consume fix, applied to `folders.ts`'s README\nabsorption); §7 (`Prop` — now abstract, `key` only, with `StringProp` its one concrete leaf\ntoday, mirroring `BaseEdge`/`Assertion` — `orderedList`/`startIndex`/`checked` *all* migrate onto\nit, none left behind); §5 (frontmatter realized as one `props` entry, not a bespoke field, once\n§7 ships); §8 (list adoption rule, the `orderedList`/`startIndex` rename, the anti-pattern\nauthoring guidance, and both projection and reconciliation mechanics — the latter needing no\ncode changes at all).",
    "title": "00C6MH0GCG02W",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:17:46.661Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MH0GCG02Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MH0GCG02Z",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Deferred deliberately**: inline features (§4) confirmed permanently out of scope — no future\nrevisit implied. (§3, blockquote, is no longer in this bucket — see \"Implemented and verified\"\nabove; it was decided and shipped this turn, not deferred.)",
    "title": "00C6MMB19801W",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MH0GCG030",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MH0GCG030",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Required once §2 ships**: §9 (`project.ts` changes for `heading`/`listItem`).",
    "title": "00C6MMB19801X",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MMB190016",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MMB190016",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C6MMB198000",
      "BlockNode/00C6MMB198001",
      "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R00Y"
    ],
    "text": "**Scope: `ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode` both, decided.** A `README.md`'s own frontmatter (if it\nhas any) extracts into `FolderNode.props` via the exact same mechanism as an ordinary artifact's\nfrontmatter → `ArtifactNode.props` — not an `ArtifactNode`-only feature. This falls out for\nclose to free: `parseMarkdownTree`'s signature change (below) already has to touch `folders.ts`'s\nREADME call site regardless (it's the same function), so handling both node kinds is barely more\nwork than handling one.",
    "title": "## 5. YAML frontmatter as node props — opaque for now, extensible later",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MMB190017",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MMB190017",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Scope: `ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode` both, decided.** A `README.md`'s own frontmatter (if it\nhas any) extracts into `FolderNode.props` via the exact same mechanism as an ordinary artifact's\nfrontmatter → `ArtifactNode.props` — not an `ArtifactNode`-only feature. This falls out for\nclose to free: `parseMarkdownTree`'s signature change (below) already has to touch `folders.ts`'s\nREADME call site regardless (it's the same function), so handling both node kinds is barely more\nwork than handling one.",
    "title": "00C6MMB190017",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MMB198000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MMB198000",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Confirmed parsing shape**: `remark-frontmatter` (now installed, tested, not yet wired into\n`astParser.ts`) with `.use(remarkFrontmatter, ['yaml'])` recognizes a leading `---\\n...\\n---`\nblock as its own mdast node, `{ type: 'yaml', value: '<raw yaml body, delimiters stripped>' }` —\nconfirmed live against a real multi-key example. Critically, this only *isolates* the block; it\ndoes **not** parse the YAML into an object — `.value` is still just a string (e.g.\n`\"title: My Doc\\ntags: [foo, bar]\\ndraft: true\"`). Turning that into real key/value data needs a\nYAML parser too (e.g. the `yaml` or `js-yaml` package — neither installed yet).",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G00Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MMB198001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MMB198001",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R00W",
      "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R00X"
    ],
    "text": "**Shape of the change, not yet built**:",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G010",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false,
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MMB198001/props/StringProp/gxsBaUkhxVUZdpo3",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MMB198001/props/StringProp/oNLYXF4jUiBDqDMD",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MMB198002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MMB198002",
    "children": [],
    "title": "00C6MMB198002",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "list",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MMB198003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MMB198003",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Frontmatter is file-level metadata, not a block — it should never become a `BlockNode` at all\n  (parallel to how `folders.ts` already treats a directory's own `README.md` specially, never\n  exposing it as a separate `ArtifactNode`). `parseMarkdownTree`'s signature would need to\n  change: return the parsed props alongside the root `BlockNode`, not fold them into the tree.\n  That ripples into every caller — `artifacts.ts`'s `ingestArtifact` (→ `ArtifactNode.props`)\n  and `folders.ts`'s README handling (→ `FolderNode.props`, per the scope decision above), plus\n  `verifyPhase0.ts`.",
    "title": "00C6MMB198003",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MMB198004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MMB198004",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Frontmatter is file-level metadata, not a block — it should never become a `BlockNode` at all\n  (parallel to how `folders.ts` already treats a directory's own `README.md` specially, never\n  exposing it as a separate `ArtifactNode`). `parseMarkdownTree`'s signature would need to\n  change: return the parsed props alongside the root `BlockNode`, not fold them into the tree.\n  That ripples into every caller — `artifacts.ts`'s `ingestArtifact` (→ `ArtifactNode.props`)\n  and `folders.ts`'s README handling (→ `FolderNode.props`, per the scope decision above), plus\n  `verifyPhase0.ts`.",
    "title": "00C6MMB198004",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MMB198005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MMB198005",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Realized through §7's unified `props` mechanism, not a bespoke per-node-kind field.** An\n  earlier revision of this doc proposed a dedicated `ArtifactNode.props: Optional xsd:string`\n  field specifically for frontmatter. Superseded: §7 designs one generic `props` mechanism for\n  *any* node kind's extensible metadata, and frontmatter is simply one more user of it — a\n  single entry (e.g. `{key: \"frontmatter\", value: \"<raw yaml body, as-is>\"}`) in whichever\n  node's (`ArtifactNode` or `FolderNode`) own `props` list, not a field of its own. Still opaque\n  for this stage (the raw\n  body, not JSON-parsed — no YAML-parsing dependency needed yet), same reasoning as before, just\n  expressed through the shared mechanism instead of a one-off field.",
    "title": "00C6MMB198005",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MMB198006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MMB198006",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Realized through §7's unified `props` mechanism, not a bespoke per-node-kind field.** An\n  earlier revision of this doc proposed a dedicated `ArtifactNode.props: Optional xsd:string`\n  field specifically for frontmatter. Superseded: §7 designs one generic `props` mechanism for\n  *any* node kind's extensible metadata, and frontmatter is simply one more user of it — a\n  single entry (e.g. `{key: \"frontmatter\", value: \"<raw yaml body, as-is>\"}`) in whichever\n  node's (`ArtifactNode` or `FolderNode`) own `props` list, not a field of its own. Still opaque\n  for this stage (the raw\n  body, not JSON-parsed — no YAML-parsing dependency needed yet), same reasoning as before, just\n  expressed through the shared mechanism instead of a one-off field.",
    "title": "00C6MMB198006",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MMB198007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MMB198007",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Not implemented: needs §7's schema addition, `remark-frontmatter` wired into the pipeline, and\n`parseMarkdownTree`'s signature (and callers — `artifacts.ts`'s `ingestArtifact`,\n`verifyPhase0.ts`) updated to extract and return the frontmatter body separately from the\n`BlockNode` tree.",
    "title": "00C6MMB198007",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MMB198013",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MMB198013",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Two implementation-checklist items, not open design questions**: (1) no shared\n`getProp(node, key)`/`setProp(...)` helper is designed yet — every call site (`astParser.ts`\nwriting, `project.ts` reading, any future query tool) needs one consistent way to look up a\n`StringProp` by `key` and decode its `value`, rather than each reinventing the scan; worth\nsettling its exact signature during implementation rather than after multiple call sites already\ndisagree. (2) `graphql.ts`'s `blockFieldSelection` needs `props { key value }` added to its\nfield-selection string — flagged proactively because forgetting exactly this (a new field never\nadded to GraphQL selection, silently reading back as `undefined`) has already happened twice\nthis session, for `type` and then for `ordered`/`start`/`checked`.",
    "title": "00C6MMB198013",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MMB198016",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MMB198016",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Scope of \"immediately precedes,\" precisely**: the list's previous sibling *within its current\nparent's children, at the point `groupByHeadings` has already run* (so a heading's own nested\ncontent already sees its restructured, non-flat sibling list) — checked once per list, against\nthe original parse structure, not recomputed after other adoptions have already happened at the\nsame level.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G023",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MMB198017",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MMB198017",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Interaction with §2's consuming rule — explicit, not just implied.** `heading` and `listItem`\nare equal here too: when a list's immediate predecessor is exactly the leading paragraph that\n§2 just consumed into that heading/listItem's own `text`, the list adopts into the\n*consumer* — the heading/listItem itself — not into nothing. A consumed-away paragraph still\ncounts as a valid adoption anchor via whatever absorbed it; it doesn't make the list orphaned\njust because the specific node it originally sat next to no longer exists as its own entry in\n`children`. Concretely, for `## Heading\\n\\nIntro sentence.\\n\\n- item one\\n- item two`: the\nleading paragraph \"Intro sentence.\" consumes into `heading.text` per §2, and the list right\nafter it adopts into the *heading*, so `heading.children` becomes the two `listItem`s directly\n— no intermediate `list` node, exactly as if the heading were a `listItem` in the already-given\nnested example below. This is the same rule stated twice for two symmetric cases, not two\ndifferent rules — worth stating explicitly rather than leaving it to be inferred from only the\n`listItem` example, which is the ambiguity that prompted this clarification.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G024",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MMB198018",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MMB198018",
    "children": [],
    "text": "This also covers the common nested-list case: `- Parent item\\n  - Nested item` today produces\n`listItem(\"Parent item\") → [paragraph, list → [listItem(\"Nested item\")]]`; under adoption, since\nthe nested list's immediately preceding sibling *is* `listItem`'s own leading paragraph (already\nconsumed into `listItem.text` per §2), the nested list's own `listItem`s become direct children\nof the outer `listItem` — no intermediate `list` node for the ordinary single-nested-list-\nunder-a-bullet case at all.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G025",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6MMB19801S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6MMB19801S",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Decided, not yet implemented**: §1 (uniform title/text semantics); §2 (heading/listItem\nconsume, shallow — supersedes the copying version shipped earlier this session); §4's\n`BlockNode.links` scope (any link with an internal-code target, either bracket form — no\nwikilink plugin needed *ever*, since the bare form only exists as a Human UI input convenience,\nnever in saved Markdown); §6 (same copy-not-consume fix, applied to `folders.ts`'s README\nabsorption); §7 (`Prop` — now abstract, `key` only, with `StringProp` its one concrete leaf\ntoday, mirroring `BaseEdge`/`Assertion` — `orderedList`/`startIndex`/`checked` *all* migrate onto\nit, none left behind); §5 (frontmatter realized as one `props` entry, scoped to *both*\n`ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode`, not a bespoke field, once §7 ships); §8 (list adoption rule —\nincluding its interaction with §2's consuming rule, now stated explicitly for both `heading` and\n`listItem` rather than left implicit — the `orderedList`/`startIndex` rename, the anti-pattern\nauthoring guidance, and both projection and reconciliation mechanics, the latter needing no code\nchanges at all).",
    "title": "00C6MMB19801S",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T20:47:26.851Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
