  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS80007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS80007",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Schema initialization — done.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER007",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS80008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS80008",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Artifact tracking & on-demand ingestion (`kg:track`/`kg:ingest`) — done, re-verified live against the new fractal-ontology schema.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER008",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS80009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS80009",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Fixed**: `kg:ingest` used to double-ingest `README.md`. `listArtifactFiles` (`artifacts.ts`) walked every `.md` file with no README exclusion, so `ingestAllArtifacts` ingested a directory's `README.md` as an ordinary `ArtifactNode`+`BlockNode` tree — in addition to, and completely independently of, `folders.ts`'s `buildFolderTree`, which separately parses the same file and correctly folds its content into the owning `FolderNode`. Reproduced live before fixing: a real `README.md` produced both a correct `FolderNode`-absorbed copy and a separate stray `ArtifactNode`. Root cause: `artifacts.ts` and `folders.ts` each walked `AperasKG/artifacts/` with their own file-listing logic, only one of which knew to skip README files. Fixed by factoring one shared `isReadmeFilename` predicate into `artifacts.ts` (which `folders.ts` now imports instead of its own local constant) and applying it in `listArtifactFiles` — plus the same guard added to `kg:track`'s explicit single-file path (`kgCli.ts`), which bypasses that list entirely and needed its own check.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER009",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS8000A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS8000A",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Automated tracking trigger (`AperasKG/.githooks/post-commit`, `post-index-change`) — done: `post-commit` auto-runs a scoped `kg:track` on commit, `post-index-change` auto-runs a full `kg:track` sweep on branch checkout / `git reset` (including `--hard`, which fires no hook of its own) — `post-checkout` was tried too but dropped as redundant once `post-index-change` was confirmed to fire alongside it with equivalent coverage.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS8000B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS8000B",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Temporal commit management — done (`versionControl.ts` is class-agnostic — branch/commit/diff/reset operations, unaffected by the schema redesign below).",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER00B",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS8000C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS8000C",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Cross-machine remotes — done: reciprocal SSH-tunneled TerminusDB remotes (reciprocal remote endpoints), `fetch` confirmed both directions; `push`/`pull` (reconciling the diverged histories) not yet attempted.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER00C",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS8000F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS8000F",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`AperasKG` was designed to hold both the domain artifacts and the KG itself, but the KG lives in a DB — so the earlier approach to fulfilling that second half was to associate whole-TerminusDB-volume/bundle backups (`restore.sh`, briefly stored under `AperasKG/db/`) with the domain-KG repo. That coupling was the wrong move: a git-tracked repo has no good way to diff, merge, or meaningfully version an opaque binary DB snapshot, and (per the `#2509` investigation above) those snapshots turned out not to even be portable between independent TerminusDB stores — so it wasn't a viable backup path for `AperasKG` even setting the git-coupling problem aside.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER00G",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS8000G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS8000G",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Corrected via the JSON-LD import/export round-trip (`web/src/lib/export.ts`, `kg:export`/`kg:import`, `Aperas-architecture.md` §5): `AperasKG/Apeiron/` holds the canonical graph content as plain, diffable JSON-LD text, committed to the same repo as `AperasKG/artifacts/` — an ordinary `git commit` over that directory is now the KG's backup, `kg:import` is the restore path.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER00H",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS8000H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS8000H",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`restore.sh`'s whole-volume/bundle mechanism (§6) is unaffected and stays as-is — it's TerminusDB server infrastructure (Docker-volume disaster recovery), a different, unrelated concern from how `AperasKG` records its own content. Likewise, the SSH-tunneled TerminusDB remotes (§7) are unrelated to `AperasKG` — that's cross-machine sync for the TerminusDB server itself, not something this redesign touches.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER00J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS8000P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS8000P",
    "children": [],
    "text": "The legacy flat `DocumentNode` / `BlockNode` / `SpanNode` schema was entirely eliminated.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER00Q",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS8000Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS8000Q",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Designed and implemented a universal, recursive `BlockNode` topology (Fractal Ontology) rooted in `ArtifactNode`.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER00R",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS8000R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS8000R",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Schema updated to pure JSON-LD (`schema.json`), avoiding TypeScript interface duplication.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER00S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS8000S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS8000S",
    "children": [],
    "text": "IDs & content fingerprint — **implemented and live-verified** (`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §1). `web/src/lib/snowflake.ts` generates the 64-bit Snowflake-style identifier (timestamp + machine identity w/ personal failover + sequence counter, 13-char Crockford Base32); `astParser.ts` uses it for every `blockId` instead of the old content hash. `ArtifactNode.contentHash` renamed to `fileHash` throughout (`artifacts.ts`, `schema.json`) to match §1.B's file-domain-only fingerprint — no structural/recursive hash. Verified live: cleared stale pre-redesign `ArtifactNode`/`BlockNode` docs, ran `kg:track`+`kg:ingest` across all 8 artifacts (1144 blocks total, including a 648-block single-artifact burst), zero id collisions. Also found and fixed a real pre-existing bug while verifying: `getArtifactRecord` treated a missing document's raw 404 response (a string, not a thrown error) as a valid record, so untracked artifacts silently reported \"unchanged\" instead of erroring. Reconciliation (best-effort content matching, §1.C history in Appendix F) remains unimplemented — no code needed it yet, since ingestion so far has only ever run against uningested artifacts.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER00T",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS8000T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS8000T",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Ingestion (`astParser.ts` and `artifacts.ts`) now parses Markdown into a deeply nested tree structure and commits the entire fractal tree via `ArtifactNode.root` in a single TerminusDB operation. Note: a plain `getDocument` read only ever returns `root`/`children` as reference ids, not the nested tree, so full-tree reads go through `getArtifactTreeViaGraphQL` (`graphql.ts`) instead — deliberately, not because the Document API is incapable of it (see `Aperas-architecture.md` §2's \"Read-path note\" for the full investigation, including a filed upstream bug, [terminusdb/terminusdb#2512](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/issues/2512)).",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER00V",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS8000V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS8000V",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`FolderNode` — **implemented and live-verified** (`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §4.A). `web/src/lib/folders.ts` walks `AperasKG/artifacts/`, builds one `FolderNode` per directory, absorbs each directory's own `README.md` (parsed blocks become the folder's children; the file itself is never exposed as a separate `ArtifactNode`), and references nested `FolderNode`s/`ArtifactNode`s by id. Wired into `npm run kg:ingest`, which now rebuilds the whole folder tree in the same run as artifact ingestion. Added one concrete `Assertion` class (inherits the previously-uninstantiable abstract `BaseEdge`) to `schema.json`, needed to let `crud.ts`/`woql.ts` actually write/query extrinsic edges — documented in `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2.D. `FolderNode`/`ArtifactNode` are now Snowflake-keyed (`folderId`/`artifactId`), `path` a plain mutable field — see the \"Reconciliation matching\" entry below for the full migration.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER00W",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS8000W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS8000W",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`crud.ts`/`woql.ts`/`graphql.ts`/`verifyPhase0.ts` — **migrated off the dead pre-redesign schema and live-verified**. `crud.ts` now provides `insertAssertion`/`deleteAssertionsInvolvingNode` for the new `Assertion` type; `woql.ts` queries `Assertion.source`/`predicate`/`target` (node-typed, not the old flat `xsd:string` `subjectId`/`objectId`) in both directions plus one-hop impact propagation; `graphql.ts` replaced the removed `docId`-scoped block query with `getArtifactTreeViaGraphQL` (bounded-depth nested `root`/`children` selection — GraphQL has no native recursion, so depth is generated explicitly, default 10). `verifyPhase0.ts` was rewritten into a full idempotent, self-cleaning live test of the whole pipeline (AST parsing → track/ingest → GraphQL tree read → `FolderNode` ingestion → `Assertion` CRUD + WOQL traversal → temporal commit management); `npm run verify:phase0 -- --db` and `npm run build` both confirmed passing.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER00X",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS8000X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS8000X",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Discovered while wiring this up**: TerminusDB enforces referential integrity on document delete — deleting a still-referenced document (e.g. an `ArtifactNode` still listed in a `FolderNode.children`, or a `BlockNode` still pointed to by `ArtifactNode.root`) fails with a `SchemaCheckFailure`, not silently. The best-effort delete helpers in `crud.ts` (`deleteDocumentIfExists`/`deleteDocumentsIfExist`) used to swallow *all* errors on the assumption only \"not found\" could occur — that assumption was wrong and masked this failure mode; they now log instead of swallowing silently, and any caller managing referenced state (like `verifyPhase0.ts`'s cleanup) has to delete in reverse-reference order.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER00Y",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS8000Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS8000Y",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Heading levels now define tree structure** — **implemented and live-verified**. `astParser.ts` gained `groupByHeadings`, a one-pass depth-stack transform run on every flat mdast sibling list before conversion: each heading becomes the parent of everything until a heading of depth <= its own. A doc with `# Title` / `## Section A` / `## Section B` now parses as nested (`Title` containing `Section A`, `Section B`, each with their own body), not a flat list under the root. Alongside this: every `BlockNode` now carries its raw mdast `type` (needed by reconciliation's leaf/container split below), and `list`/`listItem`/`blockquote` no longer carry the full-subtree-duplicate `text` they used to (confirmed live pre-fix: a `listItem`'s `text` byte-for-byte duplicated its own paragraph child's) — containers now carry no content of their own, by design.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER00Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS8000Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS8000Z",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Reconciliation matching for re-ingestion** — **implemented and live-verified** (`Aperas-reconciliation-matching-design.md`, `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §1.B/§4.A/§4.B/Appendix G). New `web/src/lib/reconcile.ts`: a Gestalt (Ratcliff/Obershelp) tree diff — the longest-common-run algorithm behind Python's `difflib` — applied two-stage per level (Stage A anchors on leaf content by `heading XOR text`; Stage B aligns containers by type within the segments Stage A's anchors define), with ambiguous same-content duplicates declined rather than guessed. Matched `BlockNode`s reuse their old `blockId`; unmatched old ones get an explicit `tombstonedAt` (new field on `BaseNode`, inherited everywhere) rather than being silently orphaned; a block relocated to a different section is detected as a cross-parent move via the same mechanism, one level up from per-parent matching. `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` moved off `Lexical`-on-`path` to Snowflake ids (`artifactId`/`folderId`, same scheme as `BlockNode.blockId`), `path` now a plain mutable field with `title`/`text` added to `ArtifactNode` (filename / naive first-paragraph abstract, matching `FolderNode`'s existing shape) — this is what makes a file/folder rename a field update instead of a delete-and-recreate. Rename detection reuses the identical Gestalt-matching primitive one level up: on every `kg:track`/`kg:ingest` sweep, disk-only and DB-only leftover paths are matched by abstract-text similarity (`matchLeftoverByAbstract`) before falling back to plain add/remove. Wired into `ingestArtifact` (fires whenever a prior ingestion exists), `trackAllArtifacts`, and `ingestFolderTree`; `kgCli.ts` reports renamed/removed/reconciled counts alongside the existing tracked/ingested counts. Live-verified via `verifyPhase0.ts` (edit-and-reingest keeps unrelated `blockId`s stable, reports sane unchanged/added counts) and a manual rename test against a throwaway artifact (`artifactId` and `blockId`s survived a real on-disk move). Migrating the key scheme required clearing all pre-existing `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`/`BlockNode` documents first, since TerminusDB rejects a schema whose new required fields the old instance data doesn't have — the whole KG was then rebuilt from `AperasKG/artifacts/` via a full `kg:track` + `kg:ingest` sweep, per the project's disk-is-source-of-truth model.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER010",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS80010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS80010",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Artifact Projection** — **implemented and live-verified** (`Aperas-artifact-projection-design.md`, `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A mode 1 of 3). New `web/src/lib/project.ts` serializes an `ArtifactNode`'s ingested `BlockNode` tree back into Markdown — canonical regeneration, not byte-exact: headings/paragraphs/code round-trip via their raw `title`/`text`, while `list`/`blockquote` are regenerated as clean Markdown (list items always blank-line-separated regardless of original tight/loose style; blockquote `> ` re-prefixed uniformly rather than trusting what the raw text happens to carry). Needed two new captured fields on `BlockNode` — `ordered`/`start` (list numbering) and `checked` (task-list checkbox) — both `Optional`, so unlike the `artifactId`/`type` migration this needed no DB wipe. Exposed via `npm run kg:project -- <path>`. Verified two ways: `verifyPhase0.ts`'s synthetic sample (every branch: ordered/unordered/task lists, nested list, blockquote, code) round-trips with zero drift by reusing `reconcile.ts`'s own matcher as the equivalence check; and, more usefully, running the same check against all 11 real artifacts in `AperasKG/artifacts/` surfaced two genuine bugs no synthetic sample had exercised — a nested code fence's closing `` ``` `` desyncing from its opening one because only a multi-line leaf's *first* line has container-required indentation stripped (every later line keeps its literal original-file column; fixed with a `dedent` normalization), and an *indented* (4-space) code block silently degrading into an ordinary paragraph because its raw slice carries no fence to re-emit (fixed by re-fencing it). After those two fixes, 9 of 11 real artifacts round-trip with zero drift; the other 2 each show one accepted, disclosed difference (absolute code-indentation depth normalized to the minimum; indented-style converted to fenced-style) — real content changes under exact-key matching, not bugs, per the design doc §8.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER011",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS80011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS80011",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Basic Assertion Authoring** — **implemented and live-verified** (`Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md`). New `kg:assert`/`kg:assertions`/`kg:unassert` CLI commands (`kgCli.ts`) let a human or agent actually write and inspect a real `Assertion` — previously the only thing that ever called `insertAssertion` was a hardcoded synthetic edge in `verifyPhase0.ts`'s test harness. `source`/`target` accept either a full node id (direct addressing) or a bare artifact/folder path (query-based addressing, resolved via `getArtifactRecord`/the new `getFolderRecord`, artifact tried first since the two never collide) — block-level assertions stay direct-id-only; closing that gap became its own effort, see \"Agentic Query Tools\" below. New narrow `deleteAssertion` (`crud.ts`) does exact source/predicate/target deletion, distinct from the existing blunt `deleteAssertionsInvolvingNode` (kept for demo/reset cleanup only). Live end-to-end test against two real artifacts (assert → list → unassert → list-empty) surfaced one real bug: WOQL binds a literal-typed field (`predicate`, `xsd:string`) as a wrapped `{\"@type\", \"@value\"}` object rather than a plain string, unlike node-typed `source`/`target` bindings — `kg:assertions` printed `[object Object]` until fixed with an `unwrapLiteral` helper in `woql.ts`'s `queryNodeAssertions`. Never caught by `verifyPhase0.ts`'s existing Assertion test because that only checks binding presence/membership, never a literal field's actual value. Reordered from Phase 2 into Phase 0 itself (not merely Phase 1) per `Aperas-design.md`'s roadmap, so Phase 1 skill-building starts with it already available.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER012",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS80012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS80012",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Agentic Query Tools** — reordered from Phase 1 into Phase 0, design settled (`Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md`) — **implemented and live-verified**. Three CLI tools closing the block-level addressing gap Basic Assertion Authoring (above) left open, and making mode 3 of `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A concrete for the first time: `kg:tree` (a deep, title-only structural map — depth-first with an optional depth bound), `kg:unfold`/`kg:fold` (the actual breadth-first primitive: one level, full content, the first code to ever give the long-inert `BlockNode.unfolded` flag a real effect — persisted only when the target is actually a `BlockNode`; a no-op note rather than an error for an `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` target, which still displays normally), and `kg:search` (keyword/regex content search via `WOQL.re` — two unrestricted `title`/`text` queries rather than one per class, since the property names are shared across all three node classes and the id prefix alone already reveals kind). Smoke-tested against the real KG (`kg:tree`, `kg:search`, an `unfold`/`fold` round-trip on a real `BlockNode`, `kg:project`).",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER013",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS80013",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS80013",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Markdown → Fractal Tree Mapping** (`Aperas-markdown-fractal-mapping-design.md`) — **implemented and live-verified**: `heading`/`listItem` now *consume* (not copy) their leading paragraph as `text`, shallow, one node fewer per section; a `list` now dissolves into whichever `paragraph`/`listItem`/`heading` immediately precedes it (`orderedList`/`startIndex` becoming that adopting node's own `props`) rather than remaining a standalone node, orphaned only when nothing valid precedes it — including the case, found while implementing this, where two lists sit directly adjacent to each other (the second is orphaned, since a `list` itself is never a valid adoption anchor); a new generic `Prop`/`StringProp` mechanism (`BaseNode.props: Set<Prop>`, `props.ts`'s shared `getProp`/`setProp`) replaces the one-field-per-feature pattern, `orderedList`/`startIndex`/`checked` all migrated onto it; YAML frontmatter is extracted via `remark-frontmatter` and stored as one `props` entry on both `ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode` (`parseMarkdownTree` now returns `{root, frontmatter?}`); `folders.ts`'s README absorption got the same copy-to-consume fix. `project.ts`'s serializer was generalized to match: `heading`/`listItem` emit their consumed `text` before their children, and `renderChildren` scans for contiguous `listItem` runs anywhere in a node's children (not just a dedicated `list` case) using that node's own `props` for numbering. One real GraphQL wrinkle surfaced and fixed: `props: Set<Prop>` can't be queried with an inline fragment on `StringProp` (TerminusDB's generated GraphQL schema makes `Prop` its own concrete type, not an interface `StringProp` implements) — worked around via the generic `_json` field every generated type carries, parsed back into `{key, value}` by `graphql.ts`'s `normalizeProps`. `verify:phase0 -- --db` and `npm run build` both clean. Same known limitation as Artifact Projection/Agentic Query Tools above: already-ingested real artifacts won't reflect any of this until something forces their re-ingestion.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER014",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS80015",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS80015",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Fractal Ontology Enhancements** (`Aperas-fractal-ontology-implementation-plan.md` Stage 2, items 4-5): item 4 (structural extraction of inline Markdown links) reordered into Phase 0 and **implemented and live-verified** — see \"Markdown → Fractal Tree Mapping\" above (`BlockNode.links` extraction). Item 5 (AI-driven summarization during ingestion, splitting blocks intelligently into `title` vs `text`) remains unimplemented — genuinely separate, needs an LLM call during ingestion, not just parser/schema work.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER016",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS80016",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS80016",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Projection Engines (Phase 1)**: the Human UI (SolidJS read-projection, 'Hover Preview' link mechanism) remains undesigned — of the three modes in `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A, mode 1 (Artifact Projection, now covering both `ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode` — see \"Markdown → Fractal Tree Mapping\" above) is implemented and mode 3 (Agentic Interface) is designed (Phase 0, above); only mode 2 (Human UI) is still untouched. Mode 1 now also writes to disk by default (`kg:project`, `--dry-run` to print instead) rather than print-only, per `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §1.B's \"the DB becomes the source of truth\" model.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER017",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS80017",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS80017",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Assertion skill(s) (Phase 1)**: a loadable `SKILL.md` teaching a coding agent when/how to use the `kg:assert`/`kg:assertions`/`kg:unassert` CLI (built in Phase 0, above) — not yet designed.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER018",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS80018",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS80018",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Phase 2+ skills: ingestion-from-arbitrary-sources, *automated* graph search skill (the manual assertion primitive moved to Phase 0, see below), refactoring/lazy-atomization skill.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER019",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1WS80019",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1WS80019",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Reconciling separate hosts' diverged `aperas_apeiron` histories (push/pull, possibly rebase) — currently only fetched-and-inspected, not merged.",
    "title": "00C6Q3P4ER01A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R004",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`title`**: this node's own folded label. Meaningful today only where the source actually\n  supplies one (a `heading`'s literal heading line); everywhere else it's a `blockId` fallback,\n  standing in for \"nothing has summarized me yet\" (AI-driven title summarization is a separate,\n  unbuilt enhancement — `Aperas-dev-status.md`, \"Fractal Ontology Enhancements\"). That fallback\n  is itself meaningful signal (`Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md` §2), not a gap to hide.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R005",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`text`**: this node's own *immediate* content — not a cache, not a derived read-only summary\n  computed from elsewhere and bolted on after the fact. If a node has a `text`, that content is\n  *this node's*, the same way a `paragraph`'s `text` unquestionably belongs to it and to no\n  other node.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R00A",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**What this supersedes**: an earlier `astParser.ts` version (`extractAbstract` reused for\n`heading`/`listItem`, called after `children` exist) was **not** this design — it was a copy, and\na deep one. **Implemented**: `convertChildren` now performs the shallow consuming version\ndescribed here instead.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G007",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R00W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R00W",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Frontmatter is file-level metadata, not a block — it should never become a `BlockNode` at all\n  (parallel to how `folders.ts` already treats a directory's own `README.md` specially, never\n  exposing it as a separate `ArtifactNode`). `parseMarkdownTree`'s signature would need to\n  change: return the parsed props alongside the root `BlockNode`, not fold them into the tree.\n  That ripples into every caller — `artifacts.ts`'s `ingestArtifact` (→ `ArtifactNode.props`)\n  and `folders.ts`'s README handling (→ `FolderNode.props`, per the scope decision above), plus\n  `verifyPhase0.ts`.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G011",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R00X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R00X",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Realized through §7's unified `props` mechanism, not a bespoke per-node-kind field.** An\n  earlier revision of this doc proposed a dedicated `ArtifactNode.props: Optional xsd:string`\n  field specifically for frontmatter. Superseded: §7 designs one generic `props` mechanism for\n  *any* node kind's extensible metadata, and frontmatter is simply one more user of it — a\n  single entry (e.g. `{key: \"frontmatter\", value: \"<raw yaml body, as-is>\"}`) in whichever\n  node's (`ArtifactNode` or `FolderNode`) own `props` list, not a field of its own. Still opaque\n  for this stage (the raw\n  body, not JSON-parsed — no YAML-parsing dependency needed yet), same reasoning as before, just\n  expressed through the shared mechanism instead of a one-off field.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G012",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R00Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R00Y",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Implemented**: §7's schema addition, `remark-frontmatter` wired into the pipeline, and\n`parseMarkdownTree`'s signature changed to `{root, frontmatter?}`, threaded through every caller\n(`artifacts.ts`'s `ingestArtifact`, `folders.ts`'s README handling, `verifyPhase0.ts`) to extract\nand return the frontmatter body separately from the `BlockNode` tree.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G013",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R010",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**The same copy-not-consume gap §2 fixed for `heading`/`listItem` also existed here — now\n  fixed the same way.** `buildFolderTree` no longer copies via `extractAbstract`; it consumes:\n  when the README's parsed root's first child is a `paragraph`, that paragraph's `text` becomes\n  `FolderNode.text` and is dropped from `readmeChildren`, with any list *that paragraph had\n  itself adopted* (§8) promoted to `FolderNode`'s own leading children — the same consume-then-\n  adopt symmetry §8 already establishes for heading/listItem, applied one level up.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G015",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R011",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C6Q3P89001A"
    ],
    "text": "**`FolderNode` → README.md projection — implemented.** `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A\n  names this as part of Artifact Projection: *\"For `FolderNode`s, the block children are\n  serialized back out to the folder's `README.md`.\"* `graphql.ts`'s new\n  `getFolderTreeViaGraphQL` and `project.ts`'s new `serializeFolderToReadme`/\n  `projectFolderToReadme` close this — `kg:project` now accepts either an artifact or a folder\n  path (tried in that order, same as everywhere else), and full round-trip is live-verified\n  (`verifyPhase0.ts` step 6b: project → write → re-ingest → project again reproduces the exact\n  same output, a stable fixed point).",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G016",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R01F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R01F",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Implemented**: schema addition (`Prop`/`StringProp`, `BaseNode.props`), a shared `getProp`/\n`setProp` helper (`props.ts`, used by `astParser.ts`, `project.ts`, `folders.ts`, `artifacts.ts`),\nand the full migration (§8/§9's projection mechanics, below). `StringProp` needs its own\n`@key: {\"@type\": \"Random\"}` (confirmed live — TerminusDB rejects a `@subdocument` class with no\nkey strategy at all, even though nothing outside the owning node ever needs to address a `Prop`\ndirectly); `Prop` itself stays keyless since it's `@abstract` and never instantiated.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G01T",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R01G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R01G",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**GraphQL representation, confirmed live — no inline fragment.** `props: Set<Prop>` can't be\nqueried as `props { key ... on StringProp { value } }`: TerminusDB's auto-generated GraphQL\nschema materializes `Prop` as its own concrete `OBJECT` type (confirmed via introspection —\n`possibleTypes: null`), not an interface/union `StringProp` implements, so `value` (a\n`StringProp`-only field) is simply absent from `Prop`'s GraphQL type — asking for it via a\nfragment errors (\"objects of type Prop can never be of type StringProp\"). The fix is `_json`,\nan escape hatch every generated GraphQL type carries: `props { key _json }` returns each prop's\nfull subdocument as a JSON string (`value` included), which `graphql.ts`'s `normalizeProps`\nparses back into the ordinary `{key, value}` shape every other reader already expects. Anything\nreading `props` through GraphQL (only `graphql.ts`'s artifact-tree read path does — the CLI's\n`kg:tree`/`kg:unfold`/`kg:search` all use the plain Document API instead) needs this same\n`_json`-then-parse step; the plain Document API's own `getDocument` needs no such workaround —\nit already returns each prop's `key`/`value` directly.",
    "title": "00C6NCTH0G01G",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T21:39:27.046Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R01N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R01N",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Two lists directly adjacent to each other — the second is orphaned, not merged into the\nfirst's adoption.** \"Immediately precedes\" is checked against the literal raw sibling right\nbefore a given list, and a `list` is never itself a valid anchor (only `paragraph`/`listItem`/\n`heading` are). So a bullet list directly followed by an ordered list — nothing but a blank line\nbetween them, a common Markdown pattern — does not have the second list adopt into whatever the\nfirst list adopted into: by the time the second list is reached, its own immediately-preceding\nraw sibling is the first `list` itself, not a valid anchor, so it stays its own orphaned node.\nThis isn't just tidy — a node can only ever host one list's `orderedList`/`startIndex` pair (see\nbelow), so letting a second, unrelated list merge into the same adoption would silently corrupt\none of the two lists' own numbering.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G026",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R01T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R01T",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`paragraph`**: structurally can't happen at all. A paragraph never has children of its own\n  except an adopted list — nothing else ever becomes a paragraph's child — so its `children` is\n  either empty or *entirely* one run. There's no \"after\" to worry about.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G02B",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R01V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R01V",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**`heading`/`listItem`**: *can* have other genuine children (sub-headings, more prose) after\n  an adopted list, since these node kinds do have real structural children beyond what any list\n  contributes — but doing so is a discouraged anti-pattern: once a section's content becomes\n  a list, further prose at the same level reads better as a new section (a following heading)\n  than as more content trailing after a list under the same one. Worth a warning to authors, not\n  a reason to complicate the algorithm — the general \"scan every position\" design already\n  handles it correctly if it happens anyway, which is the right way round: correct by\n  construction for the exceptional case, without needing to special-case *for* it.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G02C",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R01X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R01X",
    "children": [],
    "text": "§2's consuming rule required `project.ts`'s `serializeBlock` to change for `heading`/\n`serializeListItem` — under the old *copying* model, the leading paragraph was still there to\nrecurse into via `renderChildren`; under the *consuming* model it no longer exists as a child at\nall, so both cases now emit `node.text` explicitly before rendering the rest of `children`. This\nshipped in the same change as §2 itself, not after, since skipping it would have silently\ndropped the leading sentence from every projected document.",
    "title": "## 9. Consequences for Artifact Projection — implemented",
    "type": "heading",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R020",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R020",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Known limitation, inherited from precedent, not new**: `kg:track`'s hash-gate means real\nartifacts already ingested before this change won't pick up the new heading/listItem text,\n`props`-based `orderedList`/`checked`, or frontmatter until something changes their file content\nand forces re-ingestion — the same limitation already disclosed for the earlier heading/listItem\nabstract fix (`Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md` §2) and the `table`/`remark-gfm` wiring\nabove. Not fixed here, by the same reasoning: parser-version isn't part of the change-detection\nkey, and forcing a bulk re-ingest is a separate, disruptive operation from a parser-logic fix.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G02H",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R021",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R021",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Implemented and verified — Agentic Query Tools** (`Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md`):\n`kg:tree`, `kg:unfold`/`kg:fold`, `kg:search` all live in `kgCli.ts`, smoke-tested against the\nreal KG. `kg:unfold`/`kg:fold` work uniformly against any node kind for *display* (title/text of\nthe node and its immediate children), but only persist `unfolded` when the target is actually a\n`BlockNode` (the only kind the field exists on) — a no-op note, not an error, otherwise.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7R000",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R022",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R022",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Pre-existing, confirmed not new**: `BlockNode.links` extraction (§4's last subsection) —\nalready tracked in `Aperas-dev-status.md`, scope clarified earlier, still unimplemented. Out of\nthis doc's implementation scope (never listed among the \"decided, ready to build\" items above).",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG02K",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R023",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R023",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Investigated, real gap, not yet designed**: §6's second finding — `FolderNode` → `README.md`\nprojection doesn't exist at all (only `ArtifactNode` projection does), despite being named in\n`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A. Separate effort from the copy-not-consume fix above, not\npart of this implementation pass.",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG02M",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6N1XN0R024",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6N1XN0R024",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Deferred deliberately**: inline features (§4) confirmed permanently out of scope — no future\nrevisit implied.",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG02N",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR00Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6NBYGKR00Q",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Open questions, found on audit — genuinely undecided, not just unbuilt:**",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG00Q",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR00R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6NBYGKR00R",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**What does \"the inner text\" resolve *against*?** \"Treat the inner text as a target reference\"\n  never says what a \"target reference\" actually is — a `blockId`? Some other identifier this doc\n  never defines? And what happens when it doesn't resolve (a typo, or a forward reference to a\n  node that hasn't been ingested yet)? Two different shapes are equally plausible: best-effort/\n  deferred resolution, or a hard ingestion warning/failure.",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG00R",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR00S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6NBYGKR00S",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**What goes in `BaseLink.predicate` for an extracted inline link?** The field exists on the\n  schema (reused from the `Assertion` design, where it holds things like `impacts`/`verifies`),\n  but nothing here says what an *inline Markdown link's* predicate should be — a generic constant\n  like `links_to`? The link's own visible text? Left unset?",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG00S",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR00T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6NBYGKR00T",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Does extraction touch `text` at all?** §1 is explicit that a leaf's `text` is a raw,\n  unmodified source slice — which argues the link stays embedded in `text` untouched, with\n  `BaseLink` as a purely additive index. But this is never stated for links specifically, and\n  `BaseLink` has no field recording *where* in `text` the link sits (an offset, an inline id) —\n  worth confirming explicitly is the intended model, since it's inferred, not stated.",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG00T",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR00V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6NBYGKR00V",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Multiple links in one block, and `Set` vs. `List` ordering.** `BlockNode.links: Set<BaseLink>`\n  gives no ordering guarantee. If a block ever needs to know \"which link is where\" relative to\n  its siblings, `Set` semantics can't provide that — not addressed either way.",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG00V",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR00W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6NBYGKR00W",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**No stated consequences for reconciliation or projection**, unlike every other feature in this\n  doc (§9/§10 exist specifically because consuming/adoption needed them). Probably a non-issue\n  (links live inside `text`, which both `reconcile.ts` and `project.ts` already treat as an\n  opaque raw string), but that's an inference, not something this doc actually confirms.",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG00W",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR017",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6NBYGKR017",
    "children": [],
    "text": "**Open questions, found on audit:**",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG017",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR018",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6NBYGKR018",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`FolderNode` isn't a `BlockNode` — it has no mdast `type` for `serializeBlock`'s per-type\n    dispatch to key on. How does its own consumed `text` get re-emitted as a leading sentence:\n    synthesize a fake `paragraph` wrapper before calling `renderChildren`, or write a dedicated\n    folder-level entry point that special-cases `text` the way `buildFolderTree`'s ingestion side\n    special-cases the README's first child? Not decided.",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG018",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR019",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6NBYGKR019",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Does `FolderNode.props`' frontmatter entry (§5) round-trip back into a re-emitted `---` block\n    at all? Neither this doc nor `Aperas-artifact-projection-design.md` (written before `props`/\n    frontmatter existed) says — and it's the same open question for `ArtifactNode` projection,\n    just unavoidable to resolve once folder projection is being built regardless.",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG019",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR01A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6NBYGKR01A",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Read path: does this need a new `getFolderTreeViaGraphQL` paralleling\n    `getArtifactTreeViaGraphQL`, or a generalized \"get any node's tree\" function? Not specified —\n    and given §7's confirmed GraphQL-polymorphism finding (below), a naive `FolderNode.children`\n    GraphQL query wouldn't even work as a simple parallel: `children: List<BaseNode>` would only\n    ever surface `links`/`props`/`tombstonedAt` per child, never the concrete `BlockNode`/\n    `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` fields the projector actually needs — the plain Document API (the\n    same choice already made for `kg:tree`/`kg:unfold`, see `Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md`)\n    is the only currently-confirmed-working read path for this.",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG01A",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR01B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6NBYGKR01B",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Does `kg:project` on a folder path print to stdout only (today's `ArtifactNode` behavior), or\n    actually write `README.md` back to disk? The ontology doc's own wording (\"serialized back out\n    to the folder's `README.md`\") implies writing a file, which nothing built so far has ever\n    done.",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG01B",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR01C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6NBYGKR01C",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`FolderNode.children` mixes the README's own `BlockNode`s with references to nested\n    `FolderNode`/`ArtifactNode` documents (`folders.ts`'s `buildFolderTree` concatenates\n    `readmeChildren` and `structuralChildren`). Presumably a folder-level serializer skips\n    non-`BlockNode` children entirely — but `serializeBlock`'s `switch` has no guard for that\n    today, and nothing states it explicitly.",
    "title": "00C6NSQDHG01C",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-08-30T23:11:07.301Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR01W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6NBYGKR01W",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR01X",
      "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR01Y",
      "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR01Z"
    ],
    "text": "**This generalizes — confirmed via introspection on `BaseNode`/`BaseLink`/`BaseEdge`, not\n`Prop`-specific.** Every `@abstract` class in this schema, when it's the type of some field\n(`Set`/`List`/`Optional`), hits the identical restriction: its GraphQL object exposes only the\nfields defined *directly on that abstract class itself* — never a concrete leaf's own additional\nfields — with no fragment escape, only `_json`. Concretely, in this schema:",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G01W",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false,
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR01W/props/StringProp/Aj7e2HZ4cCCn3Ob6",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR01W/props/StringProp/JIMwiAOFt5SlZ6zr",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR01X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6NBYGKR01X",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`BaseNode` (fields: `links`, `props`, `tombstonedAt` only — not `blockId`/`title`/`text`/`path`,\n  which live on the concrete subclasses `BlockNode`/`ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`).",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G01X",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR01Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6NBYGKR01Y",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`BaseLink` (adds `predicate`, `target` — both defined on `BaseLink` itself, so these two work\n  fine without any fragment/`_json` workaround).",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G01Y",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR01Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6NBYGKR01Z",
    "children": [],
    "text": "`BaseEdge` (adds `source`, likewise defined on `BaseEdge` itself, also fine as-is).",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G01Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NBYGKR020",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6NBYGKR020",
    "children": [],
    "text": "Nothing currently queries `BaseNode.links: Set<BaseLink>` via GraphQL, so `source`/`target`/\n`predicate` being directly queryable there is moot today — but it will matter once `BlockNode.links`\nextraction (§4) ships and something needs to read it back. More immediately relevant:\n**`FolderNode.children: List<BaseNode>` has never been queried via GraphQL at all**, and per the\nrule above, a naive attempt would only ever surface `links`/`props`/`tombstonedAt` per child —\nnever the `blockId`/`title`/`text` (`BlockNode`), `path`/`artifactId` (`ArtifactNode`), or\n`folderId` (`FolderNode`) fields any real reader actually needs, since those all live on the\nconcrete subclasses, not `BaseNode`. This is exactly why `kgCli.ts`'s `kg:tree`/`kg:unfold`\ndeliberately read folder trees via the plain Document API (`client.getDocument`) instead of\nGraphQL — confirmed, now, to be a necessary choice rather than an incidental one.\n`ArtifactNode.root`/`BlockNode.children` are unaffected by any of this, since `BlockNode` itself\nis concrete, not abstract.",
    "title": "00C6Q98B7G020",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "unfolded": false
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode/00C6NC42X0000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "blockId": "00C6NC42X0000",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode/00C6NC42X0001"
    ],
    "title": "Document Root",
